var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/#Available-options","page":"Overview","text":"Up: pack()\n\nEvery keyword of pack(), grouped, with its default and a link to its page. A keyword page lists the choices for that keyword and says which is the default. A choice that has settings of its own, such as Bidisperse(), links on to a page of its own.","title":"Available options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/#Particles-and-contacts","page":"Overview","text":"Who the particles are and how they touch.\n\nKeyword Sets Default Choices\ndimension 2D disks or 3D spheres 2 3\nparticles_count the number of particles 256 any N ≥ 2\nparticles_size_distribution how particle diameters are drawn Bidisperse() Bidisperse(), UniformPolydisperse(), Monodisperse()\nparticles_diameters an explicit list of diameters nothing a vector\ncontact the contact force law Hookean() Hookean(), Hertzian()\nfriction tangential contact forces nothing CundallStrack(), Mindlin()\nmass the particle masses EqualMass() EqualMass(), Density()\ntarget the pressure the compression runs to Pressure(1e-4) Pressure()","title":"Particles and contacts"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/#Box,-start-and-compression","page":"Overview","text":"The box and its boundary, the particles' starting positions, and how the box is shrunk to the target pressure.\n\nKeyword Sets Default Choices\nboundary periodic boundaries or rigid walls Periodic() Periodic(), Walls()\ninitial_state where the compression starts Dilute(packing_fraction = 0.25) Dilute()\nprotocol how the box is compressed IsotropicCompression(strain_step = 1e-2) IsotropicCompression()","title":"Box, start and compression"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/#Stopping,-failure-and-restarts","page":"Overview","text":"When a run stops, what happens if it does not converge, and how to continue it.\n\nKeyword Sets Default Choices\nconvergence when a run counts as converged RelativeForce() RelativeForce(), LeidenAcceleration(), KineticEnergy(), RestartTest(), Your own test function\npressure_tolerance how close the pressure must come to the target 1e-4 any value\nsteps_max the step cap per call 10^7 any value\nfailure_action what happens when a run does not converge :warn :error\nrestart_packing continue from a saved run nothing path to a file\nrestart_file save the run state during the run nothing RestartFile()","title":"Stopping, failure and restarts"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/#Algorithm,-damping-and-time-step","page":"Overview","text":"How the packing is relaxed. damping, drag and integrator are not keywords of pack(): they are keywords of the algorithm, so they sit one level down, under algorithm.\n\nKeyword Sets Default Choices\nalgorithm the minimizer or dynamics FIRE() FIRE(), DampedDynamics(); inside them: damping, drag, integrator\ntimestep the size of the time step CollisionTime() CollisionTime(), fixed number","title":"Algorithm, damping and time step"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/#Rattlers","page":"Overview","text":"Which particles are rattlers and whether they are removed.\n\nKeyword Sets Default Choices\nrattlers which particles are rattlers and whether to remove them ContactCount() ContactCount(), TwoContact(), ForceFloor(), Unresolved(), Your own rule function, nothing","title":"Rattlers"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/#Reproducibility-and-outputs","page":"Overview","text":"The random seed and what is recorded during the run.\n\nKeyword Sets Default Choices\nseed the random seed 1 any integer\nsnapshots record the configuration during the run nothing Snapshots()\nmovie render a movie during the run nothing Movie()","title":"Reproducibility and outputs"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/#Where-is-damping?","page":"Overview","text":"note: damping, drag and integrator are keywords of the algorithm, not of `pack()`\nThe algorithm decides whether there are dashpots at all. FIRE() takes damping and drag and defaults both to nothing. DampedDynamics() takes the same two keywords and defaults damping to ContactDamping(), and it also takes integrator, which defaults to :centered_velocity_verlet.\n\npack(                                              # run the packing\n    algorithm = DampedDynamics(                    # damped dynamics\n        damping    = ContactDamping(normal = 0.98),   # contact dashpots, normal ratio 0.98\n        drag       = nothing,                         # no Stokes drag\n        integrator = :centered_velocity_verlet,       # dashpot on the end-of-step velocity (default)\n    )                                              # end of DampedDynamics call\n)                                                  # end of pack call\n\ndamping: the dashpot models ContactDamping(), ViscousRate() and RelativeVelocityDamping().\ndrag: Stokes drag, StokesDrag().\nintegrator: the time stepper, :centered_velocity_verlet, :lagged_velocity_verlet or :semi_implicit_euler.\nFIRE(): the default algorithm, with no damping and no drag.","title":"Where is damping?"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/#Beyond-the-keywords","page":"Overview","text":"PackParams(): bundle keywords into a reusable value and call pack(params).\nPacking: what pack() returns.\nfind_rattlers(): apply a rattler rule to a finished packing.\nYour own test function and Your own rule function: any function can be a convergence test or a rattler rule.\nwrite_packing(): save a packing to disk.","title":"Beyond the keywords"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/units/#Units-and-length-unit","page":"Units and length unit","text":"Up: SoftPack\n\nSoftPack works in reduced units: the numbers in a run are pure numbers, and a physical unit is attached only at the end, when a result is compared with an experiment.\n\nEvery other unit follows from three references and the contact-law exponent alpha of U(delta) = (kalpha)delta^alpha: alpha = 2 for Hookean contacts and 52 for Hertzian ones.\n\nQuantity General Hooke (alpha = 2) Hertz (alpha = 52) Default\nlength d_textref, the small diameter of the mixture   1\nmass m_textref: the EqualMass mass, or the mean mass with Density   1\nstiffness k: the stiffness of the contact law   1\nforce kd_textref^alpha-1 kd_textref kd_textref^32 1\nenergy kd_textref^alpha kd_textref^2 kd_textref^52 1\npressure kd_textref^alpha-D kd_textref^2-D: k in 2D, kd_textref in 3D kd_textref^12 in 2D, kd_textref^-12 in 3D 1\ntime sqrtm_textrefd_textref^2-alphak sqrtm_textrefk sqrtm_textrefd_textref^-12k 1\n\nEvery unit has the value 1 at the defaults, so a run quotes its numbers without a unit. The table says what a number means: it changes with the contact law only when k ne 1 or d_textref ne 1, and then the same target pressure means a different overlap under the two laws.\n\nThe packing fraction and the coordination number are dimensionless and do not depend on the units at all, which is why they are the numbers a run reports first.","title":"Units and length unit"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/units/#Changing-the-length-unit","page":"Units and length unit","text":"A run with every diameter multiplied by lambda and the same k and m is the same packing: varphi and z do not change, and only the time unit changes, by lambda^D2 for a mass that follows the volume (lambda in 2D, lambda^32 in 3D) or not at all for an equal mass. That invariance is what lets a packing made in reduced units be quoted at any physical size.\n\nFor a physical mapping, choose three numbers and derive the rest: for disks of 1 mm with k = 100 textNm and m = 1 textg, the time unit is sqrt10^-3100 texts approx 3 textms and the 2D pressure unit is kd = 10^5 textNm; the same numbers for spheres give 10^5 textPa.","title":"Changing the length unit"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/references/#References","page":"References","text":"Up: SoftPack\n\nEvery source cited on this site or in a theory page on the wiki, generated from references.bib. Each entry ends with the pages that use it: the site pages by name, the design doc, and any page outside this site. python3 docs/scripts/citations.py regenerates and checks those lists.\n\n[Agnolin2007] Ivana Agnolin, Jean-Noël Roux (2007). Internal states of model isotropic granular packings. I. Assembling process, geometry, and contact networks. Physical Review E 76, 061302. doi:10.1103/physreve.76.061302. Cited in: design doc, pack, ContactDamping, damping, DampedDynamics, algorithm, Periodic, boundary, KineticEnergy, RelativeForce, RestartTest, convergence, Dilute, initial_state, pressure_tolerance, IsotropicCompression, protocol, Unresolved, Pressure, target, Notation.\n[Bitzek2006] Erik Bitzek, Pekka Koskinen, Franz Gähler, Michael Moseler, Peter Gumbsch (2006). Structural Relaxation Made Simple. Physical Review Letters 97, 170201. doi:10.1103/physrevlett.97.170201. Cited in: design doc, pack, FIRE, algorithm.\n[CodeAster] CodeAster developers. *Algorithmes d'intégration directe de l'opérateur DYNAVIBRA / BASECALCUL='PHYS', manual R5.05.02*. <https://code-aster.org/doc/v16/manr/r5/r5.05.02.pdf>. Section 6.2, central differences. Accessed 2026-11-24. Cited in: design doc, integrator.\n[EDEMtimestep] Altair Engineering. Time Steps, EDEM documentation. https://help.altair.com/EDEM/topics/edem_simulator/understanding_time_steps_c.htm. Accessed 2026-11-18. Cited in: design doc.\n[Gilabert2007] F. A. Gilabert, J.-N. Roux, A. Castellanos (2007). Computer simulation of model cohesive powders: Influence of assembling procedure and contact laws on low consolidation states. Physical Review E 75, 011303. doi:10.1103/physreve.75.011303. Cited in: design doc, pack, ContactDamping, RelativeForce, ContactCount, rattlers.\n[Golshan2023] Shahab Golshan, Peter Münch, Rene Gassmöller, Martin Kronbichler, Bruno Blais (2023). Lethe-DEM: an open-source parallel discrete element solver with load balancing. Computational Particle Mechanics 10, 77–96. doi:10.1007/s40571-022-00478-6. Cited in: design doc, laggedvelocityverlet.\n[GronbechJensen2013] Niels Grønbech-Jensen, Oded Farago (2013). A simple and effective Verlet-type algorithm for simulating Langevin dynamics. Molecular Physics 111, 983–991. doi:10.1080/00268976.2012.760055. Cited in: design doc.\n[Guenole2020] Julien Guénolé, Wolfram G. Nöhring, Aviral Vaid, Frédéric Houllé, Zhuocheng Xie, Aruna Prakash, Erik Bitzek (2020). Assessment and optimization of the fast inertial relaxation engine (fire) for energy minimization in atomistic simulations and its implementation in lammps. Computational Materials Science 175, 109584. doi:10.1016/j.commatsci.2020.109584. Cited in: design doc, pack, FIRE, algorithm.\n[HDF5jl] JuliaIO. HDF5.jl documentation. https://juliaio.github.io/HDF5.jl/stable/. Accessed 2026-11-18. Cited in: design doc, pack, compression, write_packing, path.\n[Hanley2016] K. J. Hanley, C. O'Sullivan (2016). Analytical study of the accuracy of discrete element simulations. International Journal for Numerical Methods in Engineering 109, 29–51. doi:10.1002/nme.5275. Cited in: design doc.\n[Henkes2010] S. Henkes, M. van Hecke, W. van Saarloos (2010). Critical jamming of frictional grains in the generalized isostaticity picture. EPL (Europhysics Letters) 90, 14003. doi:10.1209/0295-5075/90/14003. Cited in: design doc.\n[Ishima2023] Daisuke Ishima, Kuniyasu Saitoh, Michio Otsuki, Hisao Hayakawa (2023). Theory of rigidity and numerical analysis of density of states of two-dimensional amorphous solids with dispersed frictional grains in the linear response regime. Physical Review E 107, 054902. doi:10.1103/physreve.107.054902. Cited in: design doc, pack, TwoContact.\n[JuliaRandom] The Julia Project. Random Numbers, The Julia Language manual (Reproducibility section). https://docs.julialang.org/en/v1/stdlib/Random/. Accessed 2026-11-18. Cited in: design doc, pack, seed.\n[JuliaStyle] The Julia Project. Style Guide, The Julia Language manual. https://docs.julialang.org/en/v1/manual/style-guide/. Accessed 2026-11-18. Cited in: design doc, Naming rules.\n[KruggelEmden2008] H. Kruggel-Emden, M. Sturm, S. Wirtz, V. Scherer. Selection of an appropriate time integration scheme for the discrete element method (DEM). Computers & Chemical Engineering. doi:10.1016/j.compchemeng.2007.11.002. Cited in: .\n[LAMMPSgran] LAMMPS developers. LAMMPS documentation: pair_style gran/hooke, gran/hooke/history, gran/hertz/history. https://docs.lammps.org/pair_gran.html. Accessed 2026-11-18. Cited in: design doc, pack, Walls.\n[LAMMPSminfire] Julien Guénolé, Erik Bitzek, LAMMPS developers. LAMMPS source code: src/min_fire.cpp. https://github.com/lammps/lammps/blob/9ce13d52969247824d3e2575c3672e6605d7da9e/src/min_fire.cpp. Commit 9ce13d5 (2026-07-10). Accessed 2026-11-20. Cited in: .\n[LAMMPSminmodify] LAMMPS developers. LAMMPS documentation: min_modify command. https://docs.lammps.org/min_modify.html. Accessed 2026-11-20. Cited in: .\n[LAMMPSminstyle] LAMMPS developers. LAMMPS documentation: min_style command. https://docs.lammps.org/min_style.html. Accessed 2026-11-20. Cited in: .\n[LAMMPSreadrestart] LAMMPS developers. LAMMPS documentation: read_restart command. https://docs.lammps.org/read_restart.html. Accessed 2026-11-19. Cited in: design doc, pack, restart_packing.\n[LAMMPSrestart] LAMMPS developers. LAMMPS documentation: restart command. https://docs.lammps.org/restart.html. Accessed 2026-11-19. Cited in: design doc, pack, RestartFile, restart_file.\n[LAMMPSset] LAMMPS developers. LAMMPS documentation: set command. https://docs.lammps.org/set.html. Accessed 2026-11-18. Cited in: design doc, pack, Density, mass.\n[LAMMPSwriterestart] LAMMPS developers. LAMMPS documentation: write_restart command. https://docs.lammps.org/write_restart.html. Accessed 2026-11-19. Cited in: design doc.\n[MATLABh5read] The MathWorks. h5read: Read data from HDF5 dataset, MATLAB documentation. https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/h5read.html. Accessed 2026-11-18. Cited in: design doc, pack, write_packing, path.\n[Martys1999] N. S. Martys and others (1999). Velocity Verlet algorithm for dissipative-particle-dynamics-based models of suspensions. Physical Review E 59, 3733. doi:10.1103/PhysRevE.59.3733. Cited in: design doc.\n[NAFEMSstable] Javier Rodriguez Soler, Joaquin Marti. The Stable Time Increment. NAFEMS Impact, Shock and Crash knowledge base KBISCWG1. https://www.nafems.org/publications/resource_center/knowledge-base-iscwg/stable-time-increment/. Accessed 2026-11-24. Cited in: design doc.\n[OHern2003] Corey S. O’Hern, Leonardo E. Silbert, Andrea J. Liu, Sidney R. Nagel (2003). Jamming at zero temperature and zero applied stress: The epitome of disorder. Physical Review E 68, 011306. doi:10.1103/physreve.68.011306. Cited in: design doc, benchmarks, Jamming scaling at zero temperature, pack, Hertzian, Hookean, contact, dimension, EqualMass, mass, particles_count, Bidisperse, Monodisperse, particlessizedistribution, Notation.\n[OSullivan2004] Catherine O'Sullivan, Jonathan D. Bray (2004). Selecting a suitable time step for discrete element simulations that use the central difference time integration scheme. Engineering Computations 21, 278–303. doi:10.1108/02644400410519794. Cited in: design doc, CollisionTime, timestep.\n[Rougier2004] E. Rougier, A. Munjiza, N. W. M. John (2004). Numerical comparison of some explicit time integration schemes used in DEM, FEM/DEM and molecular dynamics. International Journal for Numerical Methods in Engineering 61, 856–879. doi:10.1002/nme.1092. Cited in: design doc.\n[Samiei2013] K. Samiei, B. Peters, M. Bolten, A. Frommer (2013). Assessment of the potentials of implicit integration method in discrete element modelling of granular matter. Computers & Chemical Engineering 49, 183–193. doi:10.1016/j.compchemeng.2012.10.009. Cited in: design doc, DampedDynamics, centeredvelocityverlet, integrator, semiimpliciteuler.\n[Santos2024] A. P. Santos, Ishan Srivastava, Leonardo E. Silbert, Jeremy B. Lechman, Gary S. Grest (2024). Protocol-dependent frictional granular jamming simulations: cyclical, compression, and expansion. Frontiers in Soft Matter 3, 1326756. doi:10.3389/frsfm.2023.1326756. Cited in: design doc, pack, ViscousRate, damping, friction, Notation.\n[Schwager2007] Thomas Schwager, Thorsten Pöschel (2007). Coefficient of restitution and linear–dashpot model revisited. Granular Matter 9, 465–469. doi:10.1007/s10035-007-0065-z. arXiv:cond-mat/0701278. Cited in: .\n[Shimada2026] Masanari Shimada, Yusuke Hara, Shihori Koyama, Ryohei Seto, Maria Yokota (2026). Linear stability and viscoelastic response in weakly jammed frictional granular systems. arXiv:2608.30151. https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.30151. Cited in: design doc.\n[Shundyak2007] Kostya Shundyak, Martin van Hecke, Wim van Saarloos (2007). Force mobilization and generalized isostaticity in jammed packings of frictional grains. Physical Review E 75, 010301. doi:10.1103/physreve.75.010301. Cited in: design doc, pack, LeidenAcceleration, Mindlin, UniformPolydisperse, ForceFloor.\n[Silbert2001] Leonardo E. Silbert, Deniz Ertaş, Gary S. Grest, Thomas C. Halsey, Dov Levine, Steven J. Plimpton (2001). Granular flow down an inclined plane: Bagnold scaling and rheology. Physical Review E 64, 051302. doi:10.1103/physreve.64.051302. Cited in: design doc, pack, CundallStrack, CollisionTime, timestep, Notation.\n[Silbert2010] Leonardo E. Silbert (2010). Jamming of frictional spheres and random loose packing. Soft Matter 6, 2918–2924. doi:10.1039/c001973a. Cited in: Friction (Silbert 2010), benchmarks.\n[Somfai2005] Ellák Somfai, Jean-Noël Roux, Jacco H. Snoeijer, Martin van Hecke, Wim van Saarloos (2005). Elastic wave propagation in confined granular systems. Physical Review E 72, 021301. doi:10.1103/physreve.72.021301. Cited in: design doc, Home, pack, LeidenAcceleration, convergence, CundallStrack, Mindlin, friction, UniformPolydisperse, particlessizedistribution.\n[Somfai2007] Ellák Somfai, Martin van Hecke, Wouter G. Ellenbroek, Kostya Shundyak, Wim van Saarloos (2007). Critical and noncritical jamming of frictional grains. Physical Review E 75, 020301. doi:10.1103/physreve.75.020301. Cited in: design doc.\n[StableRNGs] JuliaRandom. StableRNGs.jl. https://github.com/JuliaRandom/StableRNGs.jl. Accessed 2026-11-18. Cited in: design doc, pack, seed.\n[Vagberg2014] Daniel Vågberg, Peter Olsson, S. Teitel (2014). Dissipation and Rheology of Sheared Soft-Core Frictionless Disks Below Jamming. Physical Review Letters 112, 208303. doi:10.1103/PhysRevLett.112.208303. arXiv:1311.4902. Cited in: design doc, pack, RelativeVelocityDamping.\n[Vyas2025] Dhairya R. Vyas, Julio M. Ottino, Richard M. Lueptow, Paul B. Umbanhowar (2025). Improved velocity-Verlet algorithm for the discrete element method. Computer Physics Communications 310, 109524. doi:10.1016/j.cpc.2025.109524. Cited in: design doc, laggedvelocityverlet.\n[Xiong2019] Fansheng Xiong, Philip Wang, Abram H. Clark, Thibault Bertrand, Nicholas T. Ouellette, Mark D. Shattuck, Corey S. O’Hern (2019). Comparison of shear and compression jammed packings of frictional disks. Granular Matter 21, 109. doi:10.1007/s10035-019-0964-9. Cited in: design doc, Friction (Silbert 2010), benchmarks, pack, KineticEnergy, convergence, ContactCount, rattlers.\n[deKlerk2022] David N. de Klerk, Thomas Shire, Zhiwei Gao, Andrew T. McBride, Christopher J. Pearce, Paul Steinmann (2022). A variational integrator for the Discrete Element Method. Journal of Computational Physics 462, 111253. doi:10.1016/j.jcp.2022.111253. Cited in: design doc.\n[h5py] The h5py developers. Datasets, h5py documentation. https://docs.h5py.org/en/stable/high/dataset.html. Accessed 2026-11-18. Cited in: design doc, pack, compression, write_packing, path.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/theory/#Theory-pages","page":"Theory pages","text":"Up: SoftPack\n\nEach option page on this site says what to type, what the default is, and why. The pages below hold the models, the derivations and the worked numbers behind those defaults. They are still on the wiki, one page per section of the design document, and each option page links to the wiki section that matches it.\n\n§ Page What it covers\n1 Interface and naming pack(), keywords, PackParams, naming rules\n2 Default packing Dimension, contact law, units, pressure, frictionless default\n3 Particle size distribution and length unit Bidisperse mixture, diameter ratio, length unit\n4 Mass EqualMass and Density, effective masses\n5 Convergence criterion Force, torque, pressure and kinetic-energy tests\n6 A packing that does not converge Time limits, restarts, restart files\n7 Algorithms and combinations FIRE and damped dynamics, allowed combinations\n8 Damping Contact dashpots, Stokes drag, critical damping, Rayleigh damping, mode decay\n9 Rattler rules Which particles are rattlers and how they are removed\n10 Reproducibility Seeds and random-number generators\n11 Outputs File formats, stored fields, snapshots and movies\n12 Time step Collision time, step size, stability\n13 Friction models Cundall–Strack and Mindlin, friction coefficient, stiffness ratio\n14 FIRE settings The FIRE 2.0 algorithm and its parameters\n\nThe symbols are on the Notation page and the sources on the References page.","title":"Theory pages"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/contact/Hertzian/#Hertzian()","page":"Hertzian()","text":"Up: contact\n\nThe Hertz law for elastic spheres and disks [OHern2003]: the energy grows as the five-halves power of the overlap (O'Hern's alpha = 52),\n\nU = frac25 k delta^52\n\nso the normal force is\n\nf_n = k delta^32\n\n. The stiffness is one prefactor for every pair, as with Hookean(), but the stiffness a contact actually presents grows with its overlap, partial f_npartial delta = tfrac32 k sqrtdelta.\n\nThat overlap dependence is why Hertzian() is the natural partner of Mindlin(), whose tangential stiffness is proportional to the normal one. A tangential model with a fixed ratio, CundallStrack(), also works with Hertzian contacts, but the ratio then refers to the normal stiffness at the current overlap.\n\nThe time step is chosen from the largest stiffness the run expects, at the largest overlap it expects to reach, so a Hertzian run is not timed from its stiffness at zero overlap.","title":"Hertzian()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/contact/Hertzian/#Options","page":"Hertzian()","text":"Option Default Meaning\nstiffness 1.0 the prefactor k in f_n = kdelta^32, in reduced units","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/contact/Hertzian/#Example","page":"Hertzian()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    contact = Hertzian(\n        stiffness = 1.0,  # prefactor in f_n = k delta^(3/2)\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.packing_fraction","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/contact/Hertzian/#References","page":"Hertzian()","text":"[OHern2003] Corey S. O’Hern, Leonardo E. Silbert, Andrea J. Liu, Sidney R. Nagel (2003). Jamming at zero temperature and zero applied stress: The epitome of disorder. Physical Review E 68, 011306. doi:10.1103/physreve.68.011306.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/notation/#Notation","page":"Notation","text":"Up: SoftPack\n\nThe symbol tables, with units per contact law. They are the same as the wiki's Notation page; the option pages define their own symbols where they first appear.\n\nEvery symbol used in the design doc and the wiki, with its units in SoftPack's reduced units (reference diameter d_textref = 1, contact stiffness k = 1, reference mass m_textref = 1; see §3, §2 and §4). The Units columns write d and m for one d_textref and one m_textref. Each page also defines its symbols where they first appear.\n\nThe units depend on the contact law through the exponent alpha of U(delta) = (kalpha)delta^alpha (alpha = 2 for Hooke, 52 for Hertz; §2). The columns give the general form and then the Hookean value; the Hertzian one follows by setting alpha = 52:\n\nQuantity General Hooke (alpha = 2) Hertz (alpha = 52)\nforce kd^alpha-1 kd kd^32\nenergy kd^alpha kd^2 kd^52\npressure, stress kd^alpha-D kd^2-D kd^52-D\ntime sqrtmd^2-alphak sqrtmk sqrtmd^-12k\n\nAt the default k = d = 1 every column reads 1, which is why a run quotes numbers without a unit and a comparison between the two laws can be made at the same target pressure. Away from it the same target number means a different overlap, and so different physics, under the two laws. SoftPack note.","title":"Notation"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/notation/#Conventions","page":"Notation","text":"gamma is a dashpot coefficient, force per velocity. It is never a rate.\nRates (inverse time) are r for contact dashpots, with gamma = m_texteffr, and a for Stokes drag.\nRayleigh damping is written boldsymbolGamma = amathbf M + bmathbf K: a is the mass-proportional constant (Stokes drag), b the stiffness-proportional one.\nc and C mean wave speed only, never damping. SoftPack does not use them.\nQuotes keep the source's own symbols, even where they clash with these. The text around the quote says what the source's symbol is in SoftPack's notation, for example Silbert et al.'s rate gamma_n, which is our r_n.","title":"Conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/notation/#Geometry-and-packing","page":"Notation","text":"Symbol Meaning Units\nD spatial dimension (2 or 3) –\nN number of particles –\nd_textref reference diameter, the length unit and the one particle scale (§3): the small diameter for Bidisperse, the mean diameter for UniformPolydisperse, the one diameter for Monodisperse, the mean of the list for particles_diameters; 1 by default d\nlangle drangle mean of the particles' diameters, frac1Nsum_i d_i: 1.2 for the default mixture, d_textref for Monodisperse, d_textref up to sampling scatter for UniformPolydisperse d\nd_i, R_i diameter and radius of particle i d\nr_ij center-to-center distance of particles i and j d\ndelta_ij overlap, delta_ij = (d_i + d_j)2 - r_ij (a contact if delta_ij  0) d\nhatmathbf n, hatmathbf t contact unit normal (from i to j) and unit tangent –\nvarphi, varphi_c packing fraction, and its value at jamming –\nz contacts per particle –\nOmega system area (2D) or volume (3D) d^D","title":"Geometry and packing"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/notation/#Contact-forces","page":"Notation","text":"Symbol Meaning Units\nk contact stiffness constant (the force unit for Hookean contacts) k\nalpha contact-law exponent, U(delta) = (kalpha)delta^alpha: 2 for Hooke, 5/2 for Hertz –\nU contact potential energy kd^alpha: kd^2 (Hooke)\nf_ij, f_n normal contact force, f = kdelta^alpha-1 kd^alpha-1: kd (Hooke)\nlangle f_nrangle mean normal contact force over all contacts kd\nk_n, k_t normal and tangential contact stiffness k\nF_n^textel, F_t^textel elastic normal and tangential contact forces kd\nmu Coulomb friction coefficient –\nnu Poisson ratio of the particle material (Mindlin) –","title":"Contact forces"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/notation/#Stress-and-pressure","page":"Notation","text":"Symbol Meaning Units\nsigma_alphabeta stress tensor (virial form, §2) kd^alpha-D: kd^2-D (Hooke)\nP pressure, P = tfrac1Doperatornametrboldsymbolsigma kd^alpha-D: kd^2-D (Hooke)\nP_texttarget target pressure kd^alpha-D: kd^2-D (Hooke)","title":"Stress and pressure"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/notation/#Mass-and-time","page":"Notation","text":"Symbol Meaning Units\nm_textref reference mass, the mass unit and the one mass scale (§4): the EqualMass value, or the mean mass for Density and given masses; 1 by default m\nm, m_i particle mass; m is the common mass of EqualMass m\nI_i moment of inertia of particle i, I_i = kappam_i R_i^2 md^2\nkappa inertia coefficient of a uniform particle: 12 for a disk (2D), 25 for a sphere (3D). [Agnolin2007]'s Hertz stiffness parameter kappa in §2 is a different quantity, in their notation –\nM_n normal effective mass, 1M_n = 1m_i + 1m_j m\nM_t tangential effective mass, 1M_t = 1m_i + 1m_j + R_i^2I_i + R_j^2I_j m\nm_texteff pair (normal) effective mass in the rate conventions, equal to M_n m\ntau time unit, tau = sqrtm_textrefd_textref^2-alphak: sqrtm_textrefk (Hooke) tau\nt_textcol binary collision time (§12) tau\nDelta t integration time step tau","title":"Mass and time"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/notation/#Damping-and-modes","page":"Notation","text":"Symbol Meaning Units\ngamma, gamma_n, gamma_t dashpot coefficient (normal, tangential): force per velocity sqrtk m (Hooke)\ngamma_textcrit critical coefficient, gamma_textcrit = 2sqrtk M for that direction sqrtk m\nzeta damping ratio, zeta = gammagamma_textcrit –\nr, r_n, r_t contact damping rate, gamma = m_texteffr (ViscousRate, RelativeVelocityDamping) 1tau\na, a_v, a_s Stokes drag rate (on velocity, on spin): the amathbf M term 1tau\nb, b_t stiffness-proportional constant, b = gammak per contact (tangential b_t = gamma_tk_t) tau\nboldsymbolGamma damping matrix sqrtk m\nmathbf M mass matrix m\nmathbf K, mathbf K_0 stiffness matrix (Hessian), and its unstressed part k\nmathbf K_t tangential-spring part of mathbf K_0 k\nmathbf K_perp contact sum with the tangential projector mathbf I - hatmathbf nhatmathbf n^top (frictionless; no spring behind it) k\nell mode index –\nomega_ell, lvert v_ellrangle undamped mode angular frequency and unit (mass-weighted) eigenvector 1tau, –\nlambda_ell damped eigenvalue, mathbf r(t) propto e^lambda_ell t 1tau\nbeta_ell mode decay rate, beta_ell = -operatornameRelambda_ell 1tau\nzeta_ell mode damping ratio, zeta_ell = beta_ellomega_ell –\nomega_c single-contact angular frequency, omega_c = sqrtk_nM_n 1tau\ne restitution coefficient –","title":"Damping and modes"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/notation/#Convergence","page":"Notation","text":"Symbol Meaning Units\nmathbf F_i, mathbf T_i net force and net torque on particle i kd, kd^2\nepsilon_F force (and torque) tolerance, tolerance –\nepsilon_P pressure tolerance, pressure_tolerance –\nepsilon_K kinetic-energy tolerance (option) –\nE_i^textkin kinetic energy of particle i kd^2\n\nFIRE's own parameters (mixing parameter, time-step factors, delay) are listed on §14.","title":"Convergence"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/notation/#Symbols-with-two-meanings","page":"Notation","text":"These clash between sources. Each page says which meaning it uses.\n\nSymbol Meanings\nalpha contact-law exponent; FIRE mixing parameter (§14); a tensor index in sigma_alphabeta\nsigma stress tensor sigma_alphabeta; contact distance sigma_ij in [OHern2003]'s potential\nd one d_textref in the Units columns; the dimension in [OHern2003]'s quotes (SoftPack writes D)\nP pressure. FIRE's power mathbf Fcdotmathbf v is W here; the FIRE papers and LAMMPS call it P (§14)\nbeta mode decay rate beta_ell; a tensor index in sigma_alphabeta\nboldsymbolomega particle spin (bold, a vector); omega (plain) an angular frequency\ngamma SoftPack: coefficient. LAMMPS, [Silbert2001] and [Santos2024]: a rate (our r)","title":"Symbols with two meanings"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/mass/Density/#Density()","page":"Density()","text":"Up: mass\n\nThe mass follows from a material density and the particle's size [LAMMPSset]:\n\nm_i = rho fracpi d_i^24 quad text(2D) qquad m_i = rho fracpi d_i^36 quad text(3D)\n\nThe mass reference m_textref is the mean of the masses, so a polydisperse mixture keeps the same average inertia as an equal-mass run and the time step stays comparable. With rho = 1 the small particle (d = 1) has mass 0.785 and the large one (d = 14) mass 1.539 in 2D.\n\nDensity() is the physical choice when the mixture has more than one size and the dynamics of the two species should follow their volume, for example when comparing with a granular experiment made of one material.","title":"Density()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/mass/Density/#Options","page":"Density()","text":"Option Default Meaning\ndensity 1.0 the material density rho, in reduced units\n\nBoth Density() and Density(density = 2.0) work.\n\nThere is no shape keyword: the particle shape follows the dimension, disks in 2D and spheres in 3D. Other shapes are on the Roadmap.","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/mass/Density/#Example","page":"Density()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    mass = Density(\n        density = 1.0,  # m_i = rho pi d_i^2/4 in 2D\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.masses","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/mass/Density/#References","page":"Density()","text":"[LAMMPSset] LAMMPS developers. LAMMPS documentation: set command. https://docs.lammps.org/set.html. Accessed 2026-11-18.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/#convergence","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nThe convergence test decides when a minimization has finished [Agnolin2007], [Somfai2005], [Xiong2019], and it is the setting that costs the most: the default tolerance of 10^-8 takes about 16 times the steps of 10^-4 for a packing fraction that moves in the fourth decimal.\n\nEvery test is asked after the algorithm has stepped, and it is asked together with the pressure band: a compression step is accepted only when the test holds and the pressure is inside the band. A test that never holds runs until steps_max and the run reports converged = false.\n\nDefault: RelativeForce()","title":"convergence"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default Asks\nRelativeForce() default every net force below tolerance times the mean contact force, and with friction every torque similarly\nLeidenAcceleration()  every acceleration below tolerance in reduced units\nKineticEnergy()  the force test plus a mean kinetic energy below tolerance * P * d^D\nRestartTest()  the force test, then that a restart of the packing does not grow back\nYour own test function  whatever you write, as a function of the RunState","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/#Example","page":"Overview","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    convergence = LeidenAcceleration(\n        tolerance = 1e-10,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.converged","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/#References","page":"Overview","text":"[Agnolin2007] Ivana Agnolin, Jean-Noël Roux (2007). Internal states of model isotropic granular packings. I. Assembling process, geometry, and contact networks. Physical Review E 76, 061302. doi:10.1103/physreve.76.061302.\n[Somfai2005] Ellák Somfai, Jean-Noël Roux, Jacco H. Snoeijer, Martin van Hecke, Wim van Saarloos (2005). Elastic wave propagation in confined granular systems. Physical Review E 72, 021301. doi:10.1103/physreve.72.021301.\n[Xiong2019] Fansheng Xiong, Philip Wang, Abram H. Clark, Thibault Bertrand, Nicholas T. Ouellette, Mark D. Shattuck, Corey S. O’Hern (2019). Comparison of shear and compression jammed packings of frictional disks. Granular Matter 21, 109. doi:10.1007/s10035-019-0964-9.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/mass/EqualMass/#EqualMass()","page":"EqualMass()","text":"Up: mass\n\nEvery particle has the same mass, m = 1 by default [OHern2003]. The mass reference m_textref is that mass, so the run's reduced units are the ones of a single monodisperse species.","title":"EqualMass()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/mass/EqualMass/#Why-it-is-the-default","page":"EqualMass()","text":"Equal masses are the usual choice for frictionless packings, where rotation does not enter. They also make contact damping simpler: with the matched damping ratio gamma_n = 2zeta_nsqrtk_n M_n, the reduced mass M_n is the same for every pair and the dashpot constant is proportional to sqrtk_n, so a run with equal masses and one contact law has a stiffness-proportional damping, Gamma propto K.","title":"Why it is the default"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/mass/EqualMass/#Options","page":"EqualMass()","text":"Option Default Meaning\nmass 1.0 the mass of every particle, in reduced units\n\nBoth EqualMass() and EqualMass(mass = 2.0) work.","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/mass/EqualMass/#Example","page":"EqualMass()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    mass = EqualMass(\n        mass = 1.0,  # every particle has mass 1\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.masses[1]","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/mass/EqualMass/#References","page":"EqualMass()","text":"[OHern2003] Corey S. O’Hern, Leonardo E. Silbert, Andrea J. Liu, Sidney R. Nagel (2003). Jamming at zero temperature and zero applied stress: The epitome of disorder. Physical Review E 68, 011306. doi:10.1103/physreve.68.011306.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"benchmarks/#Benchmarks","page":"Overview","text":"SoftPack is checked against the packing literature, not only against its own tests. This section collects the comparisons, the figures and the numbers behind them.\n\nThe checks answer three questions a reader should ask before trusting a packing:\n\nDoes SoftPack reach the same jamming packing fraction and coordination number as the standard frictionless packings? The reference is [OHern2003].\nDoes it show the same pressure and contact-number scaling as the jamming point is approached? The pressure should vanish as Deltavarphi for Hookean contacts and as Deltavarphi^32 for Hertzian ones; the excess coordination should grow as Deltavarphi^12.\nDoes friction move the packing to the same loose states? The reference is [Silbert2010], with the compression-protocol values of [Xiong2019] for the 2D comparison.","title":"Benchmarks"},{"category":"section","location":"benchmarks/#The-default-packing","page":"Overview","text":"(Image: Compression of the default 256-disk packing)\n\nThe default pack() run: 256 frictionless Hookean disks, 50:50 bidisperse with diameter ratio 1.4, compressed from varphi = 025 to P = 10^-4. The frame is the periodic box; blue and orange mark the two diameters. The packing takes under a second; rendering and encoding this movie adds a few seconds.\n\nThe run ends at varphi = 08387 and z = 4016 after 12 rattlers are removed, against the literature values varphi_J approx 084 and z_J = 4 [OHern2003].","title":"The default packing"},{"category":"section","location":"benchmarks/#What-is-compared","page":"Overview","text":"Check System SoftPack Reference Result\nJamming fraction 2D bidisperse, Hookean 0.8408 0.842 [OHern2003] finite-size offset, 0.001\nJamming fraction 3D bidisperse, Hookean 0.6434 0.648 [OHern2003] finite-size offset, 0.005\nPressure exponent 2D Hookean 1.04 1.01 [OHern2003] within 3 per cent\nPressure exponent 2D Hertzian 1.52 1.50 [OHern2003] within 2 per cent\nPressure exponent 3D Hookean 0.97 1.03 [OHern2003] within 6 per cent\nPressure exponent 3D Hertzian 1.32 1.51 [OHern2003] noisy, see below\nCoordination exponent 2D, Hookean / Hertzian 0.51 / 0.56 0.49 / 0.48 [OHern2003] within 0.08\nCoordination exponent 3D, Hookean / Hertzian 0.49 / 0.45 0.47 / 0.49 [OHern2003] within 0.04\nFriction, 3D mu = 0 to 1 varphi = 0637 to 0562 0639 to 0556 [Silbert2010] within 0.013\n\nThe full method, the raw rows and the analysis are in benchmarks/paper_figures. The runs use the default FIRE() and RelativeForce(1e-8); the 2D systems use N = 1024 and the 3D systems N = 512, with 2 seeds in 2D and up to 8 in 3D.","title":"What is compared"},{"category":"section","location":"benchmarks/#Pages","page":"Overview","text":"Jamming scaling (O'Hern 2003): the pressure and coordination scaling, with the published exponents.\nFriction (Silbert 2010): the friction sweep, the random-close to random-loose range, and the compression-versus-decompression difference.","title":"Pages"},{"category":"section","location":"benchmarks/#What-the-checks-do-not-claim","page":"Overview","text":"A packing is a protocol-dependent object. The comparisons use the same protocol family as the papers where the paper defines it; where it does not, the difference is stated on the page. Finite-size scatter at N = 512 is 0002 to 0004 in varphi, which is why the 3D exponents are quoted with a range rather than as exact numbers. The point of the section is to show that SoftPack lands on the published values and scaling within those uncertainties, not that every run reproduces a published seed.","title":"What the checks do not claim"},{"category":"section","location":"benchmarks/#References","page":"Overview","text":"[OHern2003] Corey S. O’Hern, Leonardo E. Silbert, Andrea J. Liu, Sidney R. Nagel (2003). Jamming at zero temperature and zero applied stress: The epitome of disorder. Physical Review E 68, 011306. doi:10.1103/physreve.68.011306.\n[Silbert2010] Leonardo E. Silbert (2010). Jamming of frictional spheres and random loose packing. Soft Matter 6, 2918–2924. doi:10.1039/c001973a.\n[Xiong2019] Fansheng Xiong, Philip Wang, Abram H. Clark, Thibault Bertrand, Nicholas T. Ouellette, Mark D. Shattuck, Corey S. O’Hern (2019). Comparison of shear and compression jammed packings of frictional disks. Granular Matter 21, 109. doi:10.1007/s10035-019-0964-9.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/semi_implicit_euler/#:semi_implicit_euler","page":":semi_implicit_euler","text":"Up: integrator\n\nThe simplest scheme [Samiei2013]: positions are updated with the new velocity, and the velocity with the force and the damping at the current step,\n\nv^n+1 = v^n + Delta t  M^-1left(F^n - Gamma v^nright) qquad x^n+1 = x^n + Delta t  v^n+1\n\nIt is explicit and first order, with the same stability limit as the lagged form, 2(sqrt1+zeta^2-zeta)omega. It is the integrator FIRE uses, and it is the one to choose when the trajectory is only a means to a relaxed state and speed per step matters more than accuracy.","title":":semi_implicit_euler"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/semi_implicit_euler/#Options","page":":semi_implicit_euler","text":"None; the integrator is chosen by name.","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/semi_implicit_euler/#Example","page":":semi_implicit_euler","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    algorithm = DampedDynamics(\n        integrator = :semi_implicit_euler,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 64,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.converged","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/semi_implicit_euler/#References","page":":semi_implicit_euler","text":"[Samiei2013] K. Samiei, B. Peters, M. Bolten, A. Frommer (2013). Assessment of the potentials of implicit integration method in discrete element modelling of granular matter. Computers & Chemical Engineering 49, 183–193. doi:10.1016/j.compchemeng.2012.10.009.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/#algorithm","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nThe algorithm moves the particles [Bitzek2006], [Guenole2020], [Agnolin2007]. SoftPack has two: FIRE, a minimization that adapts its own time step and steps back half a step on an uphill move, and damped dynamics, a physical relaxation with contact dashpots and optional Stokes drag.\n\nFIRE is the default because it reaches a tight force tolerance fastest on soft packings: in the benchmarks it converges the default run in 87,000 steps, while damped dynamics does not reach the same tolerance in five times as many. Damped dynamics is the choice when the path matters, not just the endpoint: it is a real trajectory, so its damping models, integrators and drag can be compared with a physical experiment, and its velocity field can be read at any step.\n\nBoth algorithms take the same optional damping and drag; FIRE uses them as extra dissipation on top of its own velocity mixing. They are documented under the damped-dynamics pages because that is where they are usually used.\n\nDefault: FIRE()","title":"algorithm"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default What it does\nFIRE() default adaptive-step minimization with velocity mixing\nDampedDynamics()  velocity Verlet with contact dashpots, optional drag, three integrators","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/#Example","page":"Overview","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    algorithm = DampedDynamics(\n        integrator = :centered_velocity_verlet,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 64,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.converged","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/#References","page":"Overview","text":"[Bitzek2006] Erik Bitzek, Pekka Koskinen, Franz Gähler, Michael Moseler, Peter Gumbsch (2006). Structural Relaxation Made Simple. Physical Review Letters 97, 170201. doi:10.1103/physrevlett.97.170201.\n[Guenole2020] Julien Guénolé, Wolfram G. Nöhring, Aviral Vaid, Frédéric Houllé, Zhuocheng Xie, Aruna Prakash, Erik Bitzek (2020). Assessment and optimization of the fast inertial relaxation engine (fire) for energy minimization in atomistic simulations and its implementation in lammps. Computational Materials Science 175, 109584. doi:10.1016/j.commatsci.2020.109584.\n[Agnolin2007] Ivana Agnolin, Jean-Noël Roux (2007). Internal states of model isotropic granular packings. I. Assembling process, geometry, and contact networks. Physical Review E 76, 061302. doi:10.1103/physreve.76.061302.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/find_rattlers/#find_rattlers()","page":"find_rattlers()","text":"Up: rattlers\n\nApply a rattler rule to a finished packing, without running it again:\n\nThe first argument is a Packing, the second any rule: ContactCount(), TwoContact(), ForceFloor(), Unresolved(), nothing, or a function of the packing. The result is a BitVector with one entry per particle, true for a rattler, in particle order.\n\nIt is the same code the packer runs when it applies the rule at the end of a run, so it is the way to ask \"what would a different rule say about this packing?\" without repacking. The packing's own rule is in packing.settings.rattlers, and its result is packing.rattlers.","title":"find_rattlers()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/find_rattlers/#Example","page":"find_rattlers()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    particles_count = 64,\n    seed = 1,\n)\nmask = find_rattlers(packing, ContactCount())\ncount(mask)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/snapshots/#snapshots","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nStore the particle positions at intervals during the run, so the path to the final packing can be inspected or redrawn later. A snapshot is data, not a picture: it holds the positions, and any frame can be rendered at any size afterwards with render_frame!.\n\nSnapshots live in memory during the run and are written by write_packing into the snapshots group of the HDF5 file. Nothing is stored by default (SoftPack choice).\n\nDefault: nothing","title":"snapshots"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/snapshots/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nnothing default\nSnapshots() ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/snapshots/#Example","page":"Overview","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    snapshots = Snapshots(\n        every = 10_000,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\nwrite_packing(\"packing.h5\", packing)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/drag/StokesDrag/#StokesDrag()","page":"StokesDrag()","text":"Up: drag\n\nA drag force and torque proportional to the velocity and the spin (SoftPack choice):\n\nF_textdrag = -a_v M  v qquad tau_textdrag = -a_s I  omega\n\nwith a_v and a_s rates in inverse time. Both scale with the particle's own mass or moment of inertia, so the drag does not change the relative motion of a large and a small particle, only its overall decay.\n\nduring = :always applies the drag for the whole run. during = :compression applies it while the box is being compressed and switches it off when the pressure first lands inside the band; the final minimization then runs without drag, so the packing is brought to rest by its contacts alone. That is the physically sensible choice when the drag stands in for the fluid that carried the particles in, but it leaves free particles drifting, which is why the benchmark runs use :always for a tight tolerance.","title":"StokesDrag()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/drag/StokesDrag/#Options","page":"StokesDrag()","text":"Option Default Meaning\nvelocity required a_v, the rate on the translational velocity\nspin same as velocity a_s, the rate on the spin\nduring :always :always or :compression","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/drag/StokesDrag/#Example","page":"StokesDrag()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    algorithm = DampedDynamics(\n        drag = StokesDrag(\n            velocity = 1.0,  # rate on the translational velocity\n            spin = 0.5,      # rate on the spin\n            during = :always,\n        ),\n    ),\n    particles_count = 64,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.converged","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_count/#particles_count","page":"particles_count","text":"Up: Available options\n\nHow many particles the packing has. More particles mean smaller finite-size scatter and a packing fraction closer to the thermodynamic limit [OHern2003], at a cost that grows faster than linearly: the default 2D run takes 0.7 s at 256 particles, 7.5 s at 1024 and 112.6 s at\n\n\n\nThe default is 256, which is large enough for the packing fraction to be stable to about 10^-3 and small enough to run in under a second.\n\nDefault: 256","title":"particles_count"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_count/#Options","page":"particles_count","text":"Value Meaning\nany integer N ge 2 the number of particles\n\nWith particles_diameters the count is taken from the list; the two cannot disagree.","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_count/#Finite-size-effect","page":"particles_count","text":"Default settings, seed 1, 2D:\n\nN phi z\n256 0.8387 4.016\n1024 0.8410 4.043\n4096 0.8415 4.045\n\nThe packing fraction rises with N because the small systems keep a larger fraction of their particles at the surface of the fluctuation; the rise is 0.0023 from 256 to 1024 and 0.0005 from 1024 to 4096, so quote the largest N or an ensemble.","title":"Finite-size effect"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_count/#Example","page":"particles_count","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    particles_count = 1024,  # four times the default\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.packing_fraction","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_count/#References","page":"particles_count","text":"[OHern2003] Corey S. O’Hern, Leonardo E. Silbert, Andrea J. Liu, Sidney R. Nagel (2003). Jamming at zero temperature and zero applied stress: The epitome of disorder. Physical Review E 68, 011306. doi:10.1103/physreve.68.011306.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/custom_test/#Your-own-test-function","page":"Your own test function","text":"Up: convergence\n\nconvergence also accepts a function. It is called with a RunState after each step and must return true when the packing counts as converged:\n\nThe RunState carries the live arrays of the run: positions, velocities, rotations, spins, diameters, masses, moments_of_inertia, forces, torques, the contact arrays (contact_first, contact_second, overlaps, normal_forces, tangential_forces), box_side, boundary, pressure, step and settings. The arrays are the system's own, so a function can read them freely; writing to them is possible but unsupported.\n\nThe pressure band is still applied on top of your test: a compression step is accepted only when your function returns true and the pressure is inside the band.","title":"Your own test function"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/custom_test/#Example","page":"Your own test function","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    convergence = state -> state.step >= 10_000,\n    particles_count = 64,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.converged","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/TwoContact/#TwoContact()","page":"TwoContact()","text":"Up: rattlers\n\nRemove every particle with two or fewer contacts [Ishima2023], repeating the removal until the network stops changing. It is the strict version of ContactCount(): even a frictional packing, where two contacts can hold a particle, loses its two-contact particles.\n\nIt has no cone_test: a particle with two contacts is removed whether or not its friction cones could hold it, so the test would never be reached.\n\nThis is the rule to use when the backbone should have at least three contacts per particle at every friction coefficient, for example when comparing with a measurement that defines the contact network that way.","title":"TwoContact()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/TwoContact/#Options","page":"TwoContact()","text":"None.","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/TwoContact/#Example","page":"TwoContact()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    rattlers = TwoContact(),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\ncount(packing.rattlers)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/TwoContact/#References","page":"TwoContact()","text":"[Ishima2023] Daisuke Ishima, Kuniyasu Saitoh, Michio Otsuki, Hisao Hayakawa (2023). Theory of rigidity and numerical analysis of density of states of two-dimensional amorphous solids with dispersed frictional grains in the linear response regime. Physical Review E 107, 054902. doi:10.1103/physreve.107.054902.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/Packing/#Packing","page":"Packing","text":"Up: pack()\n\npack() returns a Packing: the result of one run, in memory. It holds the analysis data, the scalar results, the full dynamic state and the provenance of the run, and it is what write_packing, find_rattlers and a restart read.","title":"Packing"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/Packing/#Per-particle","page":"Packing","text":"Each field has one entry per particle, in particle order, and the index is the particle id. N_R in the table is the number of rotational degrees of freedom: 1 in 2D and 3 in 3D.\n\nField Type Meaning\npositions D times N matrix centers, inside the box\ndiameters vector the diameter of each particle\nmasses vector the mass of each particle\nforces D times N matrix net elastic force\ntorques N_R times N matrix net elastic torque\nrattlers BitVector true for a particle the rule removed","title":"Per particle"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/Packing/#Contacts-and-results","page":"Packing","text":"Field Meaning\ncontacts the final ContactTable: pairs, walls, overlaps, forces, sliding flags\nbox_side the side of the box at the end of the run\npressure the elastic virial pressure\npacking_fraction sum_i pi d_i^D  (2^D V), all particles\ncontact_number z after the rattler removal\nforce_residual max_i lVert F_i rVert  langle f_n rangle","title":"Contacts and results"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/Packing/#Run-and-provenance","page":"Packing","text":"converged, stop_reason (:converged, :steps_max, :uphill_max, :strain_step_min), steps, compression_steps_accepted, compression_steps_rejected, strain_step_final, timestep_start, runtime_seconds, diameter_reference, diameter_mean, mass_reference, settings, seed, random_number_generator, softpack_version, softpack_commit, julia_version.","title":"Run and provenance"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/Packing/#The-state","page":"Packing","text":"system and control hold everything the run would need to continue: velocities, rotations and spins, accelerations, the contact history, the compression state and the FIRE counters. They are not meant to be read by hand; write_packing and restart_packing use them.","title":"The state"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/Packing/#Accessors","page":"Packing","text":"Function Returns\ncontact_pressure(p) the measured pressure\nmean_normal_force(p) the mean normal force over the contacts\nis_converged(p) whether the run met its test\nrun_state(p) a RunState of the final state, for a user test or rule\nfind_rattlers(p, rule) a BitVector, what another rule would say","title":"Accessors"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/Packing/#Example","page":"Packing","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\n(packing.packing_fraction, packing.contact_number)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/dimension/#dimension","page":"dimension","text":"Up: Available options\n\nThe dimension of the packing: 2 for disks in a square box, 3 for spheres in a cube. It sets the shape of a particle, the number of rotational degrees of freedom (one angle in 2D, three in 3D), the volume factor in the pressure and the expected jamming point.\n\nThe default is 2 because the shortest runs and the clearest movies are 2D. At low pressure, harmonic disks jam near phi = 084 and spheres near 064 [OHern2003]; the coordination is z_textiso = 2D frictionless and D + 1 with friction.\n\nDefault: 2","title":"dimension"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/dimension/#Options","page":"dimension","text":"Value Meaning\n2 disks in a square box\n3 spheres in a cube","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/dimension/#Example","page":"dimension","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    dimension = 3,          # 2 gives disks, 3 gives spheres\n    particles_count = 512,  # more particles for a 3D fluctuation\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.packing_fraction\n\nIn 3D use more particles than in 2D for the same quality: the surface-to-volume ratio of the fluctuation is larger, and the README example uses 512.","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/dimension/#References","page":"dimension","text":"[OHern2003] Corey S. O’Hern, Leonardo E. Silbert, Andrea J. Liu, Sidney R. Nagel (2003). Jamming at zero temperature and zero applied stress: The epitome of disorder. Physical Review E 68, 011306. doi:10.1103/physreve.68.011306.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/mass/#mass","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nThe mass sets the inertia of a particle and, with the stiffness, the elastic time scale of the run. It does not change the jammed configuration at a given pressure, only how the run gets there and how long it takes: heavier particles move more slowly under the same force [OHern2003], [LAMMPSset].\n\nWith friction the mass matters twice, because each particle also carries a moment of inertia\n\nI = kappa m R^2\n\nwith kappa = 12 for a disk and kappa = 25 for a sphere, and R = d2. The spin equation uses I, and the tangential effective mass that sets the tangential contact time includes it.\n\nDefault: EqualMass()","title":"mass"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/mass/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default Mass of particle i\nEqualMass() default m for every particle\nDensity()  rho times the area (2D) or volume (3D)","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/mass/#Example","page":"Overview","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    mass = Density(\n        density = 1.0,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.masses","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/mass/#References","page":"Overview","text":"[OHern2003] Corey S. O’Hern, Leonardo E. Silbert, Andrea J. Liu, Sidney R. Nagel (2003). Jamming at zero temperature and zero applied stress: The epitome of disorder. Physical Review E 68, 011306. doi:10.1103/physreve.68.011306.\n[LAMMPSset] LAMMPS developers. LAMMPS documentation: set command. https://docs.lammps.org/set.html. Accessed 2026-11-18.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/RelativeForce/#RelativeForce()","page":"RelativeForce()","text":"Up: convergence\n\nA minimization has converged when no particle feels a net force [Agnolin2007], [Gilabert2007]:\n\nmax_i F_i  varepsilon  langle f_n rangle\n\nwith langle f_n rangle the mean normal contact force of the configuration and varepsilon the tolerance. With friction on, each particle must also carry no net torque:\n\nfracsqrtT_i cdot T_iI_i  d_textref  varepsilon  langle f_n rangle\n\nwhere I_i is the moment of inertia and d_textref the small diameter of the mixture. Dividing by I_i and multiplying by a diameter turns the torque into the force scale it would produce, so the same varepsilon means the same thing for translation and rotation.","title":"RelativeForce()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/RelativeForce/#Why-it-is-the-default","page":"RelativeForce()","text":"This is the default test because it is the one that describes jamming directly: a jammed packing is one where every particle can be balanced by its contacts.\n\nThe default varepsilon = 10^-8 is provisional: it is kept until frictional runs have been timed, because the tail of a frictional minimization is much longer than a frictionless one.","title":"Why it is the default"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/RelativeForce/#Options","page":"RelativeForce()","text":"Option Default Meaning\ntolerance 1e-8 varepsilon, relative to the mean contact force","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/RelativeForce/#Cost","page":"RelativeForce()","text":"2D, N = 256, P = 10^-4, mean of two seeds: 10^-4 takes 55,000 steps, 10^-6 71,000 and 10^-8 87,000.","title":"Cost"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/RelativeForce/#Example","page":"RelativeForce()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    convergence = RelativeForce(\n        tolerance = 1e-8,  # max force divided by the mean contact force\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.force_residual","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/RelativeForce/#References","page":"RelativeForce()","text":"[Agnolin2007] Ivana Agnolin, Jean-Noël Roux (2007). Internal states of model isotropic granular packings. I. Assembling process, geometry, and contact networks. Physical Review E 76, 061302. doi:10.1103/physreve.76.061302.\n[Gilabert2007] F. A. Gilabert, J.-N. Roux, A. Castellanos (2007). Computer simulation of model cohesive powders: Influence of assembling procedure and contact laws on low consolidation states. Physical Review E 75, 011303. doi:10.1103/physreve.75.011303.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/friction/CundallStrack/#CundallStrack()","page":"CundallStrack()","text":"Up: friction\n\nA linear tangential spring with a Coulomb cap [Somfai2005], [Silbert2001]. The tangential force is\n\nf_t = -k_t xi\n\nwith xi the tangential displacement stored since the contact was made, and the force is clipped at mu f_n: once f_t reaches the cap the contact is sliding, the stored displacement stops growing in the direction of the cap, and the flag is reported in the contact table.\n\nThe tangential stiffness is a fixed fraction of the normal one, k_t = rk_n. The default, stiffness_ratio = :matched, gives r = 13 in 2D and r = 27 in 3D. Those are the values that make the tangential contact time equal the normal one when the tangential effective mass includes the particle's rotation, which is the mass the tangential spring actually moves; with them, the normal and tangential springs are equally stiff in time and neither sets a shorter time step than the other.\n\nThe tangential force exerts a torque on each particle, tau = -R times f_t, so rotation and spin are part of the dynamics. Friction applies during the minimization and during damped dynamics alike.","title":"CundallStrack()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/friction/CundallStrack/#Options","page":"CundallStrack()","text":"Option Default Meaning\nfriction_coefficient 0.5 mu in the Coulomb cap\nstiffness_ratio :matched k_tk_n; :matched is 13 in 2D and 27 in 3D, or give a number","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/friction/CundallStrack/#Example","page":"CundallStrack()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    friction = CundallStrack(\n        friction_coefficient = 0.3,  # mu in the Coulomb cap\n        stiffness_ratio = :matched,  # k_t/k_n = 1/3 in 2D\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.contact_number","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/friction/CundallStrack/#References","page":"CundallStrack()","text":"[Somfai2005] Ellák Somfai, Jean-Noël Roux, Jacco H. Snoeijer, Martin van Hecke, Wim van Saarloos (2005). Elastic wave propagation in confined granular systems. Physical Review E 72, 021301. doi:10.1103/physreve.72.021301.\n[Silbert2001] Leonardo E. Silbert, Deniz Ertaş, Gary S. Grest, Thomas C. Halsey, Dov Levine, Steven J. Plimpton (2001). Granular flow down an inclined plane: Bagnold scaling and rheology. Physical Review E 64, 051302. doi:10.1103/physreve.64.051302.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/restart_packing/#restart_packing","page":"restart_packing","text":"Up: Available options\n\nContinue a run from a file written by write_packing or by a RestartFile [LAMMPSreadrestart]: The stored state is loaded and the run continues from it: the compression state, the FIRE counters, the contact history and the settings all come back, so the continuation is the same run, not a new one. A restart redoes the compression trial that was in progress from the accepted state, which is why continuing a finished packing leaves it unchanged and why an interrupted run resumes exactly where it stopped.\n\nOnly run-control settings may differ from the stored ones: steps_max, failure_action, convergence, restart_file, snapshots, movie and pressure_tolerance. Any other keyword raises an error even when it equals the stored value, because passing it says you expect it to do something. An omitted steps_max starts a fresh budget counted from the restart, and a recording schedule counts every from the step the job resumed at.\n\nA restart is also the clean way to tighten a tolerance: run to 10^-4, then continue to 10^-8 without paying for the first part twice.\n\nDefault: nothing","title":"restart_packing"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/restart_packing/#Choices","page":"restart_packing","text":"Choice Default\nnothing default\npath to a file ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/restart_packing/#Example","page":"restart_packing","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    restart_packing = \"run.h5\",\n    steps_max = 10^7,\n)\npacking.converged","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/restart_packing/#References","page":"restart_packing","text":"[LAMMPSreadrestart] LAMMPS developers. LAMMPS documentation: read_restart command. https://docs.lammps.org/read_restart.html. Accessed 2026-11-19.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/friction/Mindlin/#Mindlin()","page":"Mindlin()","text":"Up: friction\n\nThe Hertz-Mindlin tangential force [Somfai2005], [Shundyak2007]. The tangential stiffness is proportional to the normal one,\n\nk_t = eta k_n qquad eta = frac2(1 - nu)2 - nu\n\nwith nu the Poisson ratio, so the tangential stiffness grows with the overlap in the same way the Hertzian normal stiffness does. The Coulomb cap and the sliding rule are the same as for CundallStrack().\n\nMindlin() needs Hertzian() contacts: with a linear normal law the ratio eta has nothing to scale. The default Poisson ratio of 0.0 gives eta = 1, equal normal and tangential stiffness at every overlap.","title":"Mindlin()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/friction/Mindlin/#Options","page":"Mindlin()","text":"Option Default Meaning\nfriction_coefficient 0.5 mu in the Coulomb cap\npoisson_ratio 0.0 nu in eta = 2(1-nu)(2-nu); must lie between -1 and 0.5","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/friction/Mindlin/#Example","page":"Mindlin()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    contact = Hertzian(),\n    friction = Mindlin(\n        friction_coefficient = 0.3,\n        poisson_ratio = 0.0,  # eta = 1, so k_t = k_n\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.contact_number","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/friction/Mindlin/#References","page":"Mindlin()","text":"[Somfai2005] Ellák Somfai, Jean-Noël Roux, Jacco H. Snoeijer, Martin van Hecke, Wim van Saarloos (2005). Elastic wave propagation in confined granular systems. Physical Review E 72, 021301. doi:10.1103/physreve.72.021301.\n[Shundyak2007] Kostya Shundyak, Martin van Hecke, Wim van Saarloos (2007). Force mobilization and generalized isostaticity in jammed packings of frictional grains. Physical Review E 75, 010301. doi:10.1103/physreve.75.010301.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/compression/#compression","page":"compression","text":"Up: write_packing()\n\nCompress the HDF5 datasets to make the file smaller [HDF5jl], [h5py]. Nothing is compressed by default, so the datasets stay memory-mappable and a reader can open a large file without decompressing all of it.\n\nChoice Default Meaning\nnothing default uncompressed\n:gzip  gzip at level 4\n(:gzip, level)  gzip at the given level, 1 to 9\n\ngzip is lossless and is applied per dataset, so a compressed file reads exactly like an uncompressed one. The price is that a dataset must be read as a whole: a compressed file cannot be memory-mapped.\n\ncompression applies to HDF5 only. With format = :csv it is ignored, because the CSV path writes text.","title":"compression"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/compression/#Example","page":"compression","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(particles_count = 64, seed = 1)\nwrite_packing(\n    \"packing.h5\",\n    packing;\n    compression = :gzip,\n)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/compression/#References","page":"compression","text":"[HDF5jl] JuliaIO. HDF5.jl documentation. https://juliaio.github.io/HDF5.jl/stable/. Accessed 2026-11-18.\n[h5py] The h5py developers. Datasets, h5py documentation. https://docs.h5py.org/en/stable/high/dataset.html. Accessed 2026-11-18.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/protocol/IsotropicCompression/#IsotropicCompression()","page":"IsotropicCompression()","text":"Up: protocol\n\nShrink every side of the box by the same factor, relax, look at the pressure, and repeat [Agnolin2007]. One step is:\n\nscale the side length, and every position with it, by 1 - varepsilon;\nrun the algorithm until the convergence test holds at the new box;\nread the pressure.\n\nIf the pressure is still below the target band, the step is accepted and the next one uses the same varepsilon. If the step overshoots the band, it is rejected: the state and the box are restored, varepsilon is halved, and the step is tried again. The halving stops at strain_step_min, below which the run reports :strain_step_min and stops.","title":"IsotropicCompression()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/protocol/IsotropicCompression/#Why-it-is-the-default","page":"IsotropicCompression()","text":"Isotropic compression is the standard assembling protocol for model granular packings [Agnolin2007]: it has no shear, so the contact network is built by the same strain path in every direction. The record is kept on the result: the default 2D run does 69 accepted and 15 rejected compression steps before it lands inside the band. Rejections are cheap, because a rejected step is undone before the expensive minimization.\n\nWith Walls the walls move with the box, so the container shrinks with the packing.","title":"Why it is the default"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/protocol/IsotropicCompression/#Options","page":"IsotropicCompression()","text":"Option Default Meaning\nstrain_step 1e-2 the first relative shrink varepsilon, between 0 and 1","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/protocol/IsotropicCompression/#Example","page":"IsotropicCompression()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    protocol = IsotropicCompression(\n        strain_step = 1e-2,  # first box shrink, one percent\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.strain_step_final","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/protocol/IsotropicCompression/#References","page":"IsotropicCompression()","text":"[Agnolin2007] Ivana Agnolin, Jean-Noël Roux (2007). Internal states of model isotropic granular packings. I. Assembling process, geometry, and contact networks. Physical Review E 76, 061302. doi:10.1103/physreve.76.061302.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"benchmarks/jamming-scaling/#Jamming-scaling-at-zero-temperature","page":"Jamming scaling","text":"Up: Benchmarks\n\n[OHern2003] measured how the pressure and the coordination number approach the jamming point for frictionless disks and spheres, with both harmonic and Hertzian contacts. The paper fits\n\nfracPd^2-Dk = p_0(varphi-varphi_c)^c qquad z-z_c = Z_0(varphi-varphi_c)^12\n\nwith c = 101 for harmonic and c = 150 for Hertzian contacts, and Z_c = 4 in 2D and 6 in 3D (their Table I). The packing fraction at jamming is varphi_J approx 0842 in 2D and 0648 in 3D for the bidisperse mixture [OHern2003].","title":"Jamming scaling at zero temperature"},{"category":"section","location":"benchmarks/jamming-scaling/#Pressure","page":"Jamming scaling","text":"(Image: Pressure versus distance to jamming)\n\nPressure against varphi-varphi_c for 2D (left) and 3D (right) bidisperse packings. Points are SoftPack runs; solid lines are fits on the asymptotic window; dotted lines have the published slopes 1.0 and 1.5.","title":"Pressure"},{"category":"section","location":"benchmarks/jamming-scaling/#Coordination","page":"Jamming scaling","text":"(Image: Coordination versus distance to jamming)\n\nExcess contact number against varphi-varphi_c. The dotted line has the published slope 0.5.","title":"Coordination"},{"category":"section","location":"benchmarks/jamming-scaling/#Numbers","page":"Jamming scaling","text":"D contact N varphi_c (SoftPack) varphi_J (paper) c (SoftPack) c (paper) z exponent (SoftPack) z exponent (paper)\n2 Hookean 1024 0.8408 0.842 1.04 1.01 0.51 0.49\n2 Hertzian 1024 0.8415 0.842 1.52 1.50 0.56 0.48\n3 Hookean 512 0.6434 0.648 0.97 1.03 0.49 0.47\n3 Hertzian 512 0.6443 0.648 1.32 1.51 0.45 0.49\n\nThe 2D exponents reproduce the paper to a few per cent. The 3D Hertzian pressure exponent is the noisiest number: the N = 512 packing fraction scatters by 0002 to 0004 between seeds, and the fit window then contains only three points. It is quoted as 13 pm 02, not as a precise value.\n\nThe pressure coefficient p_0 is not compared directly: SoftPack uses one stiffness for every pair, while [OHern2003] scale the pair stiffness by 1sigma_ij^alpha. That changes p_0 at fixed Deltavarphi but not the exponent, and it does not change the jamming packing fraction.","title":"Numbers"},{"category":"section","location":"benchmarks/jamming-scaling/#Reproduce","page":"Jamming scaling","text":"The run script, the raw CSV and the analysis script are in benchmarks/paper_figures.","title":"Reproduce"},{"category":"section","location":"benchmarks/jamming-scaling/#References","page":"Jamming scaling","text":"[OHern2003] Corey S. O’Hern, Leonardo E. Silbert, Andrea J. Liu, Sidney R. Nagel (2003). Jamming at zero temperature and zero applied stress: The epitome of disorder. Physical Review E 68, 011306. doi:10.1103/physreve.68.011306.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/contact/#contact","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nThe contact law is the normal force between two particles that overlap. SoftPack's particles are soft: an overlap delta = d_ij - r_ij (the sum of the radii minus the center distance) stores an elastic energy, and the force pushes the particles apart. The law acts only in compression; there is no attraction [OHern2003].\n\nWith friction off, the normal force is the whole contact. With friction on, the law fixes the normal stiffness that the tangential model scales from, so the two settings are chosen together: Mindlin() needs Hertzian(), while CundallStrack() works with either law.\n\nThe stiffness is one number for every pair, in the run's reduced units, where the reference mass is 1 and the reference diameter is the small diameter of the mixture. Its size sets the elastic time scale tau sim sqrtm_textrefk, and therefore the time step of the run; it does not change the jamming packing fraction.\n\nDefault: Hookean()","title":"contact"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/contact/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default Contact force\nHookean() default f = kdelta\nHertzian()  f = kdelta^32","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/contact/#Example","page":"Overview","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    contact = Hertzian(\n        stiffness = 1.0,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.packing_fraction","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/contact/#References","page":"Overview","text":"[OHern2003] Corey S. O’Hern, Leonardo E. Silbert, Andrea J. Liu, Sidney R. Nagel (2003). Jamming at zero temperature and zero applied stress: The epitome of disorder. Physical Review E 68, 011306. doi:10.1103/physreve.68.011306.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/timestep/CollisionTime/#CollisionTime()","page":"CollisionTime()","text":"Up: timestep\n\nThe starting step is a fraction of the elastic contact time of the lightest pair [Silbert2001], [OSullivan2004]. For a linear contact law the time of a half oscillation is pisqrtMk, so\n\nDelta t_0 = textfraction times pi sqrtfracMk\n\nwith M the reduced mass of the lightest pair, tfrac12mathrmmin_i m_i. For a Hertzian law the stiffness is evaluated at the largest overlap the run expects, which is estimated from the target pressure, because the stiffness at zero overlap is zero.\n\nWith friction the tangential contact can be the stiffer one, so the rule takes the smaller of the normal and tangential times,\n\nDelta t_0 = textfraction times pi mathrmminleft(sqrtfracM_nk_n sqrtfracM_tk_tright)\n\nwhere the tangential mass M_t = M_n(1 + 1kappa) includes the particle's rotation (kappa = 12 for a disk, 25 for a sphere).","title":"CollisionTime()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/timestep/CollisionTime/#Why-it-is-the-default","page":"CollisionTime()","text":"The default fraction is 150, a twentieth of the collision time, which is a safe step for FIRE's semi-implicit Euler and for the lagged integrators.","title":"Why it is the default"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/timestep/CollisionTime/#Options","page":"CollisionTime()","text":"Option Default Meaning\nfraction 1/50 the fraction of the contact time","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/timestep/CollisionTime/#A-fixed-step-instead","page":"CollisionTime()","text":"A number is read in units of tau = sqrtm_mathrmmink, the elastic time of the lightest particle, so the same number is the same physical step whatever the mass distribution; with the default stiffness and equal masses, a fixed 0.02 corresponds to CollisionTime(fraction = f) with f = 002sqrt2pi approx 00090.","title":"A fixed step instead"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/timestep/CollisionTime/#Example","page":"CollisionTime()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    timestep = CollisionTime(\n        fraction = 1/50,  # Delta t = t_col / 50\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.timestep_start","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/timestep/CollisionTime/#References","page":"CollisionTime()","text":"[Silbert2001] Leonardo E. Silbert, Deniz Ertaş, Gary S. Grest, Thomas C. Halsey, Dov Levine, Steven J. Plimpton (2001). Granular flow down an inclined plane: Bagnold scaling and rheology. Physical Review E 64, 051302. doi:10.1103/physreve.64.051302.\n[OSullivan2004] Catherine O'Sullivan, Jonathan D. Bray (2004). Selecting a suitable time step for discrete element simulations that use the central difference time integration scheme. Engineering Computations 21, 278–303. doi:10.1108/02644400410519794.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/target/#target","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nThe target is what the compression runs to [Agnolin2007]. SoftPack stops when the pressure measured from the contacts is inside a band around the target and the convergence test holds, so the target is the pressure at which the packing is defined, not a packing fraction: the packing fraction that comes out is the answer, not the input.\n\nThe pressure is the elastic virial of the contact forces divided by the volume; it counts the pair contacts and, for a Walls box, the wall contacts.\n\nDefault: Pressure(1e-4)","title":"target"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/target/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nPressure() default","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/target/#Example","page":"Overview","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    target = Pressure(1e-3),  # a looser, faster packing\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.packing_fraction","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/target/#References","page":"Overview","text":"[Agnolin2007] Ivana Agnolin, Jean-Noël Roux (2007). Internal states of model isotropic granular packings. I. Assembling process, geometry, and contact networks. Physical Review E 76, 061302. doi:10.1103/physreve.76.061302.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/Unresolved/#Unresolved()","page":"Unresolved()","text":"Up: rattlers\n\nA contact whose normal force is below the run's convergence tolerance, times the mean contact force, does not count as a contact [Agnolin2007]. The particle then needs two effective contacts and the removal cascades.\n\nTwo contacts, not the D + 1 of ContactCount() and ForceFloor(), is [Agnolin2007]'s rule: they remove a particle held by fewer than two contacts, and the cascade repeats. It is stricter than the isostatic count for a frictionless packing in 2D, so run this rule alongside ContactCount() if the isostatic count is the number you need.\n\nThe idea is that a contact below the tolerance is one the minimization did not resolve: it may be an artifact of the final numbers rather than a physical contact, so a particle held only by such contacts is not really held. Because the threshold follows the convergence tolerance, a loose run removes more particles than a tight one, which is the honest behavior: a loose run cannot tell a weak contact from a resolved one.\n\nfloor = :tolerance (the default) uses the convergence tolerance; a number overrides it.","title":"Unresolved()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/Unresolved/#Options","page":"Unresolved()","text":"Option Default Meaning\nfloor :tolerance the threshold relative to the mean force, or :tolerance\ncone_test true accepted; the cone test currently runs only for ContactCount()","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/Unresolved/#Example","page":"Unresolved()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    rattlers = Unresolved(\n        floor = :tolerance,\n        cone_test = true,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\ncount(packing.rattlers)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/Unresolved/#References","page":"Unresolved()","text":"[Agnolin2007] Ivana Agnolin, Jean-Noël Roux (2007). Internal states of model isotropic granular packings. I. Assembling process, geometry, and contact networks. Physical Review E 76, 061302. doi:10.1103/physreve.76.061302.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/overwrite/#overwrite","page":"overwrite","text":"Up: write_packing()\n\nWhether an existing file may be replaced. The default is a SoftPack choice.\n\nChoice Default Behavior\nfalse default stop with an ArgumentError if any file it would write exists\ntrue  replace the existing file\n\nThe default refuses, because a packing can take minutes and a typo that overwrites it costs the whole run. The check covers every file the call would write: the main file, the rattlers file, and both CSV files.\n\nEvery write is atomic. The file is written to a temporary file in the same directory and renamed over the target, so a job killed during a write leaves the previous file intact; with overwrite = true that means an interrupted write never leaves a half-written packing in place of a good one.","title":"overwrite"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/overwrite/#Example","page":"overwrite","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(particles_count = 64, seed = 1)\nwrite_packing(\n    \"packing.h5\",\n    packing;\n    overwrite = true,\n)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/boundary/#boundary","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nThe box that holds the particles [Agnolin2007]. A periodic box has no walls: a particle that leaves one side comes back on the opposite one, so the packing is a piece of an infinite medium and has no boundary effects. A Walls box has rigid walls on every side, so the packing has a container, a surface layer, and wall friction if the walls are frictional.\n\nThe choice changes the pressure bookkeeping (wall contacts add a term to the virial), the initial placement (walls are filled rather than wrapped), and the coordination number near the surface, but not the physics of the contact law.\n\nDefault: Periodic()","title":"boundary"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/boundary/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nPeriodic() default\nWalls() ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/boundary/#Example","page":"Overview","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    boundary = Walls(\n        friction = true,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.packing_fraction","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/boundary/#References","page":"Overview","text":"[Agnolin2007] Ivana Agnolin, Jean-Noël Roux (2007). Internal states of model isotropic granular packings. I. Assembling process, geometry, and contact networks. Physical Review E 76, 061302. doi:10.1103/physreve.76.061302.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/Bidisperse/#Bidisperse()","page":"Bidisperse()","text":"Up: particles_size_distribution\n\nA mixture of two diameters: a fixed fraction of the particles are small, and the rest are larger by a fixed ratio. It is the default particles_size_distribution, and its small diameter is SoftPack's unit of length.","title":"Bidisperse()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/Bidisperse/#The-call-with-every-option-written-out","page":"Bidisperse()","text":"pack(                                           # run the packing\n    particles_size_distribution = Bidisperse(   # two-diameter mixture (default)\n        diameter_small = 1.0,                   # small diameter; sets the length unit (default)\n        diameter_ratio = 1.4,                   # large diameter divided by small diameter (default)\n        fraction_small = 0.5,                   # number fraction of small particles (default)\n    )                                           # end of Bidisperse call\n)                                               # end of pack call\n\npack(...) — runs the packing.\nparticles_size_distribution = Bidisperse(...) (default) — a mixture of two diameters in a fixed ratio, with N_s = fN particles small and f = fraction_small.\nThe two-size mixture prevents crystallization in 2D [OHern2003].\ndiameter_small = 1.0 (default) — the small diameter, which also sets the length unit.\n\nd_i in d_s rd_s qquad d_s = textttdiameter_small\n\n[OHern2003] \"The diameter of the smaller particle is denoted by σ\" … \"lengths are measured in units of σ.\"\n\ndiameter_ratio = 1.4 (default) — the ratio r = d_ld_s of the large to the small diameter.\n[OHern2003] 50-50 mixtures \"with a size ratio of 1.4 in order to prevent crystallization.\"\nfraction_small = 0.5 (default) — the number fraction of small particles.\n\nN_s = fN text small qquad N_l = (1-f)N text large qquad f = textttfraction_small\n\nThe polydispersity (standard deviation over mean) of the default is sqrtf(1-f)(r-1)(f+(1-f)r) = 16 = 0167 (SoftPack note, from the two-diameter moments).","title":"The call with every option written out"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/Bidisperse/#Why-these-defaults","page":"Bidisperse()","text":"Why a mixture at all: equal disks crystallize in 2D, and a crystal is not the disordered packing jamming studies need.\nA second size frustrates the hexagonal order (SoftPack note).\ndiameter_small = 1.0 — the small diameter as the length unit.\n[OHern2003] \"The diameter of the smaller particle is denoted by σ.\" ... \"lengths are measured in units of σ\"\ndiameter_ratio = 1.4 and fraction_small = 0.5 — the [OHern2003] mixture.\n[OHern2003] \"In our two-dimensional (2D) simulations, we have used 50-50 mixtures of particles with a size ratio of 1.4 in order to prevent crystallization\".","title":"Why these defaults"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/Bidisperse/#Theory","page":"Bidisperse()","text":"For now these sections are on the wiki.\n\nBidisperse law\nBidisperse moments\nChanging the length unit\nBidisperse default\nWorked numbers","title":"Theory"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/Bidisperse/#Example","page":"Bidisperse()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    particles_size_distribution = Bidisperse(\n        diameter_small = 1.0,  # small diameter; sets the length unit\n        diameter_ratio = 1.4,  # large diameter divided by small\n        fraction_small = 0.5,  # half the particles are small\n    ),\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.diameters","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/Bidisperse/#References","page":"Bidisperse()","text":"[OHern2003] Corey S. O’Hern, Leonardo E. Silbert, Andrea J. Liu, Sidney R. Nagel (2003). Jamming at zero temperature and zero applied stress: The epitome of disorder. Physical Review E 68, 011306. doi:10.1103/physreve.68.011306.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#Roadmap:-other-particle-shapes","page":"Roadmap","text":"Up: SoftPack\n\nnote: Roadmap, not designed\nNothing on this page is designed, and none of it is promised for a first release. It records what is planned and what the design keeps open, so that it can be added without changing the meaning of any call made today. The decision is in section 15 of the design document, and the wiki has the same page: Roadmap.\n\nSoftPack's particles are disks in 2D and spheres in 3D, fixed by dimension (see mass).","title":"Roadmap: other particle shapes"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#Planned-shapes","page":"Roadmap","text":"Cylinders (3D disks): a disk with a thickness, in a 3D box.\nUser-defined shapes: the user passes their own shape functions, the right-hand column of the table below.\nSpheres confined to a plane, the Leiden 2D convention, would come back as one more 2D shape with sphere mass and kappa = 25.\nIt would be a shape, not a Density keyword.","title":"Planned shapes"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#What-a-shape-provides","page":"Roadmap","text":"A shape is more than a mass formula. The table lists everything in the design that assumes a disk or a sphere, with what another shape would have to supply instead.\n\nQuantity Disk and sphere today Any other shape\nSize one diameter d_i shape parameters (a cylinder: diameter and thickness)\nMass rhopi d^24 or rhopi d^36 rho times the shape's area or volume\nMoment of inertia the scalar I = kappamR^2 in 3D an inertia tensor about the center of mass\nRotation state an angle (2D) or a spin vector (3D) an orientation and a spin vector\nContact overlap delta = (d_i + d_j)2 - r_ij along the line of centers a function that returns the overlap, the contact normal and the contact point, for a pair and for a wall\nEffective masses M_n from the masses alone, M_t adds R^2I both include rotation: each particle adds (mathbf rtimeshatmathbf e)^topmathbf I^-1(mathbf rtimeshatmathbf e) to 1m, with mathbf r the vector to the contact point and hatmathbf e the force direction\nPressure a tangential force drops out because the normal lies along boldsymbolell^c in general it does not drop out\nStored output diameters and masses the shape kind, its parameters and the orientations","title":"What a shape provides"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#Rules-for-the-first-implementation","page":"Roadmap","text":"All SoftPack choice, so that the planned shapes can be added later.\n\nEvery shape-specific quantity in the table sits behind one shape interface, with the disk and the sphere as its first two members.\nNo other code writes pi d^24, R^2I or (d_i + d_j)2 - r_ij.\nThe contact laws, friction models and dashpots read the overlap, the normal, the contact point and the effective masses from the shape's contact, not the diameters.\nTorques are mathbf rtimesmathbf f about the contact point, which for a disk or a sphere is Rhatmathbf n.\nOutput and restart files record a shape kind beside each diameter, so a file written today still reads after more shapes exist.\nA new shape arrives as an addition, a new value or constructor with today's disk or sphere as the default, so no call written today changes meaning.","title":"Rules for the first implementation"},{"category":"section","location":"roadmap/#Open-questions","page":"Roadmap","text":"The contact test and the neighbor search for non-round shapes.\nHow a shape sets the length unit and the packing fraction.\nThe contact laws for non-round shapes, since the Hertz law depends on the curvatures at the contact.\nThe rattler counts.","title":"Open questions"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/lagged_velocity_verlet/#:lagged_velocity_verlet","page":":lagged_velocity_verlet","text":"Up: integrator\n\nVelocity Verlet with the damping force taken from the previous step's relative velocities [Golshan2023], [Vyas2025]. The update is explicit:\n\nv^n+1 = v^n + Delta t  M^-1left(F^n - Gamma v^n-1right)\n\nso the cost per step is a sparse matrix-vector product rather than a solve.\n\nThe lag makes the scheme stable only for a smaller step than the centered form, 2(sqrt1+zeta^2-zeta)omega instead of 2omega, and it shifts the effective damping slightly. It is useful when the damping matrix changes every step and a factorization would be wasteful, and as a reference for the effect of the lag.","title":":lagged_velocity_verlet"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/lagged_velocity_verlet/#Options","page":":lagged_velocity_verlet","text":"None; the integrator is chosen by name.","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/lagged_velocity_verlet/#Example","page":":lagged_velocity_verlet","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    algorithm = DampedDynamics(\n        integrator = :lagged_velocity_verlet,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 64,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.converged","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/lagged_velocity_verlet/#References","page":":lagged_velocity_verlet","text":"[Golshan2023] Shahab Golshan, Peter Münch, Rene Gassmöller, Martin Kronbichler, Bruno Blais (2023). Lethe-DEM: an open-source parallel discrete element solver with load balancing. Computational Particle Mechanics 10, 77–96. doi:10.1007/s40571-022-00478-6.\n[Vyas2025] Dhairya R. Vyas, Julio M. Ottino, Richard M. Lueptow, Paul B. Umbanhowar (2025). Improved velocity-Verlet algorithm for the discrete element method. Computer Physics Communications 310, 109524. doi:10.1016/j.cpc.2025.109524.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/drag/#drag","page":"Overview","text":"Up: DampedDynamics\n\nStokes drag is a body force on every particle, proportional to its velocity, as if the packing sat in a viscous fluid. The drag model is a SoftPack choice; it is the standard Stokes drag. It is separate from the contact dashpots: the dashpots act only where two particles touch, while drag acts on every particle, including one that touches nothing.\n\nDrag is the tool that fixes the long tail of damped dynamics: a free particle that the contact dashpots cannot slow down is brought to rest by the drag.","title":"drag"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/drag/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nnothing default\nStokesDrag() ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/drag/#Example","page":"Overview","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    algorithm = DampedDynamics(\n        drag = StokesDrag(\n            velocity = 1.0,\n        ),\n    ),\n    particles_count = 64,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.converged","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/Monodisperse/#Monodisperse()","page":"Monodisperse()","text":"Up: particles_size_distribution\n\nEvery particle has the same diameter, and that diameter is the unit of length. It is not the default, because equal disks crystallize in 2D.","title":"Monodisperse()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/Monodisperse/#The-call-with-every-option-written-out","page":"Monodisperse()","text":"pack(                                             # run the packing\n    particles_size_distribution = Monodisperse(   # every particle has the same diameter\n        diameter = 1.0,                           # the one diameter; sets the length unit (default)\n    )                                             # end of Monodisperse call\n)                                                 # end of pack call\n\npack(...) — runs the packing.\nparticles_size_distribution = Monodisperse(...) — every particle has the same diameter.\nEqual disks crystallize in 2D, which the bidisperse default avoids.\n[OHern2003] studied \"the same bidisperse mixture as well as monodisperse systems\" in 3D.\ndiameter = 1.0 (default) — the one diameter, which also sets the length unit.\nSoftPack choice: the one diameter is the length unit.","title":"The call with every option written out"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/Monodisperse/#Theory","page":"Monodisperse()","text":"For now these sections are on the wiki.\n\nMonodisperse law\nMonodisperse","title":"Theory"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/Monodisperse/#Example","page":"Monodisperse()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    dimension = 3,  # monodisperse disks crystallize in 2D\n    particles_count = 512,\n    particles_size_distribution = Monodisperse(\n        diameter = 1.0,  # every particle has this diameter\n    ),\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.packing_fraction","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/Monodisperse/#References","page":"Monodisperse()","text":"[OHern2003] Corey S. O’Hern, Leonardo E. Silbert, Andrea J. Liu, Sidney R. Nagel (2003). Jamming at zero temperature and zero applied stress: The epitome of disorder. Physical Review E 68, 011306. doi:10.1103/physreve.68.011306.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/boundary/Periodic/#Periodic()","page":"Periodic()","text":"Up: boundary\n\nA periodic square (2D) or cube (3D) [Agnolin2007]. A particle's image re-enters the box on the opposite side, distances are minimum-image, and a pair of particles can touch across the boundary. There is no wall, so the packing has no surface layer and every particle sees the same environment on average.\n\nThe pressure of a periodic packing is the pair virial alone,\n\nP = frac1D Vsum_textpairs f_n  l\n\nand the frame is the box, so a movie of a periodic run shows particles crossing the edge and reappearing on the far side.","title":"Periodic()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/boundary/Periodic/#Why-it-is-the-default","page":"Periodic()","text":"This is the default because it is the cleanest way to measure bulk jamming: the packing fraction of the default run, phi = 08387, is not depressed by a container.","title":"Why it is the default"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/boundary/Periodic/#Example","page":"Periodic()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    boundary = Periodic(),  # periodic square (2D) or cube (3D)\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.packing_fraction","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/boundary/Periodic/#References","page":"Periodic()","text":"[Agnolin2007] Ivana Agnolin, Jean-Noël Roux (2007). Internal states of model isotropic granular packings. I. Assembling process, geometry, and contact networks. Physical Review E 76, 061302. doi:10.1103/physreve.76.061302.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/contact/Hookean/#Hookean()","page":"Hookean()","text":"Up: contact\n\nA harmonic repulsion. The elastic energy of a contact with overlap delta  0 is\n\nU = frac12 k delta^2\n\nso the normal force is linear in the overlap,\n\nf_n = k delta\n\nand the stiffness k is the same at every overlap and for every pair. For delta le 0 the energy and the force are zero.","title":"Hookean()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/contact/Hookean/#Why-it-is-the-default","page":"Hookean()","text":"Hookean() is the default because it is the simplest law that produces a jammed packing, and because its jamming point is the one the literature has measured most often. In 2D the harmonic bidisperse mixture jams near phi_J approx 084 and in 3D near 064, with z - z_textiso to 0 as the pressure falls [OHern2003]. Those are the reference values against which a new packing, a new seed or a new code is usually checked, so making them the default gives every user the same starting point.\n\nThe law is also the cheapest to run. Because k does not depend on the overlap, every contact has the same elastic time, tau = pisqrtm_texteffk, so the time step is fixed for the whole run and the damping ratio does not change as the packing compresses. A Hertzian contact stiffens as its overlap grows, which forces a smaller step at large overlaps and makes the run more expensive for the same packing.\n\nHookean() is a model choice, not a claim that real grains have linear contacts. The difference is in the force law, not in the packing protocol: both laws jam at the same phi_J in the zero-pressure limit [OHern2003], but at a finite pressure the overlap and the pressure coefficient differ. Use Hertzian() when the contact law itself is the question, for example when comparing with an experiment or when the tangential model is Mindlin(); use Hookean() when the question is the packing and a fixed, cheap contact time is an advantage.\n\nSoftPack uses one stiffness k for every pair. [OHern2003] scale the pair stiffness by the particle size, epsilonsigma_ij^alpha; that changes the pressure coefficient at a fixed Deltaphi but not the jamming packing fraction (the design document, §2, records the difference).","title":"Why it is the default"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/contact/Hookean/#Options","page":"Hookean()","text":"Option Default Meaning\nstiffness 1.0 the spring constant k of every pair, in reduced units","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/contact/Hookean/#Example","page":"Hookean()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    contact = Hookean(\n        stiffness = 2.0,  # spring constant of every pair\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.packing_fraction\n\nDoubling the stiffness halves the elastic time scale, so the packer takes a smaller time step; the packing fraction at a given pressure is unchanged to within the run-to-run scatter.","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/contact/Hookean/#References","page":"Hookean()","text":"[OHern2003] Corey S. O’Hern, Leonardo E. Silbert, Andrea J. Liu, Sidney R. Nagel (2003). Jamming at zero temperature and zero applied stress: The epitome of disorder. Physical Review E 68, 011306. doi:10.1103/physreve.68.011306.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/initial_state/#initial_state","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nWhere the compression starts. The start has to be below the jamming packing fraction, so that the particles do not overlap and the first minimization has something to do; a dilute random placement at rest is the simplest such state [Agnolin2007] and is the one the design specifies.\n\nDefault: Dilute(packing_fraction = 0.25)","title":"initial_state"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/initial_state/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nDilute() default","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/initial_state/#Example","page":"Overview","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    initial_state = Dilute(\n        packing_fraction = 0.20,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.packing_fraction","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/initial_state/#References","page":"Overview","text":"[Agnolin2007] Ivana Agnolin, Jean-Noël Roux (2007). Internal states of model isotropic granular packings. I. Assembling process, geometry, and contact networks. Physical Review E 76, 061302. doi:10.1103/physreve.76.061302.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/movie/#movie","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nRender a movie of the run: a frame every every steps, drawn straight from the positions, encoded with ffmpeg at the end. A movie shows how the packing gets there, which a snapshot cannot; the price is one image per frame and an ffmpeg run at the end.\n\nNothing is recorded by default (SoftPack choice).\n\nDefault: nothing","title":"movie"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/movie/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nnothing default\nMovie() ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/movie/#Example","page":"Overview","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    movie = Movie(\n        \"packing.mp4\",\n        every = 1_000,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/movie/Movie/#Movie()","page":"Movie()","text":"Up: movie\n\nRecording is a SoftPack choice. Render a frame every every steps into a temporary directory and encode the frames into path when the run ends. every can also be a wall-clock period. The frame is size pixels square; fps is the frame rate of the encoded movie.\n\nThe renderer is the one of render_frame!: disks or spheres with depth fog, wrapped at the edges of a periodic box, and a thin diameter line on every particle when the packing has friction, so the rotation is visible in the movie.\n\nEncoding needs ffmpeg on the PATH. Without it the run still packs and the movie is skipped with a warning in packing.control.warnings; if encoding fails, the frame directory is kept and its path is reported, so the frames are not lost.\n\nA movie adds little to the run time: a 720-pixel frame of 256 disks takes 1.3 ms, so a frame every 500 steps over 87,000 steps costs about 0.2 s. The frames are large (about 1.5 MB each as PPM) but they live in the temporary directory and only the encoded movie is kept.","title":"Movie()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/movie/Movie/#Options","page":"Movie()","text":"Option Default Meaning\npath required the movie file; the extension picks the container ffmpeg writes\nevery required steps (an integer) or a period between frames\nsize 640 the image width in pixels\nfps 25 frames per second of the movie","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/movie/Movie/#Example","page":"Movie()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    movie = Movie(\n        \"packing.mp4\",\n        every = 500,\n        size = 720,\n        fps = 30,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/damping/RelativeVelocityDamping/#RelativeVelocityDamping()","page":"RelativeVelocityDamping()","text":"Up: damping\n\nOne dashpot on the full relative velocity, normal and tangential at once [Vagberg2014]:\n\nf_textdamp = -M r  v_textrel\n\nwith a single rate r (default 0.5). Because the force follows the velocity vector rather than the contact normal, it damps sliding and approach together and needs no separate tangential spring.\n\nIt is the simplest model to reason about and the one that most resembles a viscous fluid; it is not the model of a contact, so its damping does not scale with the stiffness.","title":"RelativeVelocityDamping()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/damping/RelativeVelocityDamping/#Options","page":"RelativeVelocityDamping()","text":"Option Default Meaning\nrate 0.5 r, the damping rate\nattractive false whether the dashpot can pull\ncoulomb_limit :elastic which normal force caps the tangential part","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/damping/RelativeVelocityDamping/#Example","page":"RelativeVelocityDamping()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    algorithm = DampedDynamics(\n        damping = RelativeVelocityDamping(\n            rate = 0.5,  # one dashpot on the full relative velocity\n        ),\n    ),\n    particles_count = 64,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.converged","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/damping/RelativeVelocityDamping/#References","page":"RelativeVelocityDamping()","text":"[Vagberg2014] Daniel Vågberg, Peter Olsson, S. Teitel (2014). Dissipation and Rheology of Sheared Soft-Core Frictionless Disks Below Jamming. Physical Review Letters 112, 208303. doi:10.1103/PhysRevLett.112.208303. arXiv:1311.4902.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/pressure_tolerance/#pressure_tolerance","page":"pressure_tolerance","text":"Up: Available options\n\nThe width of the pressure band [Agnolin2007]. A compression step is accepted only when the measured pressure is within this relative band of the target,\n\nP - P_texttarget le textpressure_tolerance times P_texttarget\n\non top of whatever the convergence test says. The default 10^-4 lands the pressure within one part in ten thousand, which is tighter than the run-to-run scatter of the packing fraction.\n\nThe band is what makes the compression protocol finite: without it the box would shrink until the pressure matched the target exactly, which no finite number of steps can do. A tighter band costs steps; a looser band saves them and lets the packing fraction wander in the fourth decimal.\n\nDefault: 1e-4","title":"pressure_tolerance"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/pressure_tolerance/#Options","page":"pressure_tolerance","text":"Value Meaning\nany positive number the relative half-width of the accepted pressure band","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/pressure_tolerance/#Example","page":"pressure_tolerance","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    pressure_tolerance = 1e-4,  # relative band around the target\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.pressure","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/pressure_tolerance/#References","page":"pressure_tolerance","text":"[Agnolin2007] Ivana Agnolin, Jean-Noël Roux (2007). Internal states of model isotropic granular packings. I. Assembling process, geometry, and contact networks. Physical Review E 76, 061302. doi:10.1103/physreve.76.061302.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#Creating-a-packing-with-pack()","page":"Overview","text":"Status: the engine implements every setting of the design; pre-release.","title":"Creating a packing with pack()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#Summary","page":"Overview","text":"pack() with no arguments makes a 2D packing of 256 frictionless Hookean disks, a 50:50 bidisperse mixture with diameter ratio 1.4, compressed with FIRE to the pressure P = 10^-4, with rattlers removed. It starts from random, non-touching particles at packing fraction 0.25 in a periodic box and shrinks the box isotropically until the pressure reaches the target. Every setting below has a default and a reason. This page lists them all in one place: the default, what it means as an equation, the other choices, the main source, and the wiki section that explains it. Sources are cited by key and listed in full at the end; SoftPack choice marks a default with no literature source.\n\nUnits throughout: reference diameter d_textref = 1 (the small diameter of the default mixture), contact stiffness k = 1, reference mass m_textref = 1, so lengths are in d, forces in kd (Hookean), pressures in kd^2-D and times in tau = sqrtm_textrefk (Units and length unit). Symbols are defined in Notation.","title":"Summary"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#The-default-packing-in-one-call","page":"Overview","text":"Every default written out:\n\npack(                                                                # run the packing with every default written out\n    dimension                   = 2,                                 # 2D packing of disks (use 3 for spheres)\n    particles_count             = 256,                               # number of particles\n    particles_size_distribution = Bidisperse(                        # two-diameter mixture\n        diameter_small = 1.0,                                        # small diameter; the length unit\n        diameter_ratio = 1.4,                                        # large diameter divided by small diameter\n        fraction_small = 0.5                                         # number fraction of small particles (50:50)\n    ),                                                               # end of Bidisperse call\n    particles_diameters         = nothing,                           # draw diameters from the distribution, not an explicit list\n    contact                     = Hookean(stiffness = 1.0),          # Hookean contact law, stiffness k = 1\n    friction                    = nothing,                           # frictionless contacts\n    mass                        = EqualMass(1.0),                    # every particle has mass 1\n    target                      = Pressure(1e-4),                    # compress until the contact pressure reaches 1e-4\n    boundary                    = Periodic(),                        # periodic square (2D) or cube (3D) box\n    initial_state               = Dilute(packing_fraction = 0.25),   # random start at packing fraction 0.25, no particles touching\n    protocol                    = IsotropicCompression(              # shrink the box in isotropic steps\n        strain_step = 1e-2                                           # the box side shrinks by 1% in the first step\n    ),                                                               # end of IsotropicCompression call\n    convergence                 = RelativeForce(tolerance = 1e-8),   # stop when net forces are below 1e-8 of the mean contact force\n    pressure_tolerance          = 1e-4,                              # relative pressure tolerance\n    steps_max                   = 10^7,                              # maximum number of steps per call\n    failure_action              = :warn,                             # warn and return the packing if not converged\n    restart_packing             = nothing,                           # start a fresh run, no restart\n    restart_file                = nothing,                           # no periodic saves during the run\n    algorithm                   = FIRE(),                            # FIRE with LAMMPS FIRE 2.0 defaults, no damping, no drag\n    timestep                    = CollisionTime(fraction = 1/50),    # time step is 1/50 of the collision time\n    rattlers                    = ContactCount(cone_test = true),    # remove particles with too few contacts, cone test on\n    seed                        = 1,                                 # random seed\n    snapshots                   = nothing,                           # no snapshots recorded\n    movie                       = nothing,                           # no movie rendered\n)                                                                    # end of pack call\n\nand, for files, write_packing(path, packing; rattlers_file = false, compression = nothing, overwrite = false), which writes HDF5 (file format).\n\npack(...) — runs the packing with every default written out.\nEach keyword is explained in the tables below: its default, meaning, other choices, main source and a link to the section with the reasons.\nParticles, contacts, mass and target: Particles and contacts.\nBox, start and compression: Box, start and compression.\nConvergence, failure and restarts: Stopping, failure and restarts.\nAlgorithm and time step: Algorithm and time step.\nDamped dynamics: Damping (damped dynamics).\nRattlers: Rattlers.\nSeed and outputs: Reproducibility and outputs.\nwrite_packing(path, packing; rattlers_file = false, compression = nothing, overwrite = false) — the file call, written with its defaults (file format).\nrattlers_file = false — write only the remaining particles (rattlers_file).\ncompression = nothing — leave the datasets uncompressed (compression).\noverwrite = false — stop with an error if a file already exists (overwrite).","title":"The default packing in one call"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#Three-ways-to-call-pack()","page":"Overview","text":"pack()                                                  # every default\npack(particles_count = 2000, target = Pressure(1e-4))   # keywords\npack(params)                                            # a reusable PackParams value\n\npack() — runs with every default.\npack(particles_count = 2000, target = Pressure(1e-4)) — runs with the keywords you pass, and every keyword you leave out keeps its default.\npack(params) — runs with a reusable settings value, described on the PackParams page.\npack() returns a Packing, described on the Packing page.","title":"Three ways to call pack()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#Where-next","page":"Overview","text":"Available options: every keyword of pack() in one place, one link each, with the choices of each keyword.\nwrite_packing(): saving the packing that pack() returns.\nTheory pages: the models, derivations and worked numbers behind the defaults.","title":"Where next"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#Particles-and-contacts","page":"Overview","text":"Setting Default Meaning Other choices Main source Details\ndimension 2 disks, D = 2 3 (spheres) SoftPack choice dimension\nparticles_count 256 N = 256 any N ge 2 SoftPack choice particles_count\nparticles_size_distribution Bidisperse(diameter_small = 1.0, diameter_ratio = 1.4, fraction_small = 0.5) half the particles have d = 1, half 14d UniformPolydisperse(diameter_mean = 1.0, spread = 0.2): d_i uniform on 08 12; Monodisperse(diameter = 1.0) [OHern2003]; [Somfai2005] for the uniform law particles_size_distribution\nparticles_diameters nothing diameters drawn from the distribution a vector, one diameter per particle SoftPack choice particles_diameters\ncontact Hookean(stiffness = 1.0) U(delta) = tfrac12 kdelta^2, f = kdelta while the overlap delta  0 Hertzian(stiffness = 1.0): U = tfrac25 kdelta^52, f = kdelta^32 [OHern2003] contact\nfriction nothing frictionless: normal forces only CundallStrack, Mindlin (next table) SoftPack choice friction\nmass EqualMass(1.0) m_i = 1 for every particle Density(1.0): m_i = rhopi d_i^24 in 2D, rhopi d_i^36 in 3D SoftPack choice; [LAMMPSset] for density masses mass\ntarget Pressure(1e-4) P = dfrac1DOmegasum_c mathbf f^ccdotboldsymbolell^c = 10^-4 (boldsymbolell^c the branch vector, Omega the box area or volume), elastic forces only any pressure [Agnolin2007] (stress formula) target\n\nWith friction on, the friction models' own defaults:\n\nSetting Default Meaning Main source Details\nCundallStrack(friction_coefficient = 0.5, stiffness_ratio = :matched) mu = 05, k_tk_n = 1(1 + mR^2I) linear tangential spring capped at lvert F_t^textelrvert le mu F_n^textel; k_tk_n = 13 for disks, 27 for spheres [Somfai2005], [Silbert2001]; [Santos2024] for the model CundallStrack()\nMindlin(friction_coefficient = 0.5, poisson_ratio = 0.0) mu = 05, nu = 0 Hertz–Mindlin tangential force (needs Hertzian); nu = 0 gives k_t = k_n [Somfai2005], [Shundyak2007] Mindlin()","title":"Particles and contacts"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#Box,-start-and-compression","page":"Overview","text":"Setting Default Meaning Other choices Main source Details\nboundary Periodic() periodic square (2D) or cube (3D) box of side L, every side scaled by the same factor Walls(friction = true): rigid flat walls, with the contact law and, when friction is set, its friction model; Walls(friction = false) for normal forces only [Agnolin2007] (periodic cell); SoftPack choice (square or cube, walls) boundary\ninitial_state Dilute(packing_fraction = 0.25) random particles at varphi_0 = 025, at rest, placed by random sequential addition so that none touches any varphi_0 at which the particles fit [Agnolin2007] (loose start); SoftPack choice (value) initial_state\nprotocol IsotropicCompression(strain_step = 1e-2) shrink the box by 1% per step and minimize; accept a step below the pressure band, reject and halve the step above it any strain_step SoftPack choice protocol","title":"Box, start and compression"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#Stopping,-failure-and-restarts","page":"Overview","text":"Setting Default Meaning Other choices Main source Details\nconvergence RelativeForce(tolerance = 1e-8) max_i lvertmathbf F_irvert  epsilon_Flangle f_nrangle and, with friction, max_i lvertmathbf T_irvert  epsilon_Flangle f_nranglelangle drangle, with epsilon_F = 10^-8 (the rest test only; the pressure row below applies to every option) LeidenAcceleration(tolerance = 1e-10), KineticEnergy(tolerance = 1e-8), RestartTest(), any function [Agnolin2007], [Gilabert2007] convergence\npressure_tolerance 1e-4 lvert P - P_texttargetrvert le epsilon_P P_texttarget, epsilon_P = 10^-4, checked after any convergence option any value [Agnolin2007] pressure_tolerance\nsteps_max 10^7 at most 10^7 steps per call any value SoftPack choice steps_max\nfailure_action :warn warn and return the packing with converged = false :error SoftPack choice failure_action\nrestart_packing nothing start from scratch path to a file written by write_packing [LAMMPSreadrestart] (precedent) restart_packing\nrestart_file nothing no state saved during the run RestartFile(path; every = interval), every required [LAMMPSrestart] (precedent) restart_file\n\nThe options' own defaults: LeidenAcceleration from [Somfai2005], KineticEnergy (mean kinetic energy per particle below epsilon_K P d^D) from [Agnolin2007] and [Xiong2019], RestartTest (zero the velocities, check the forces do not grow back) from [Agnolin2007] (§5).","title":"Stopping, failure and restarts"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#Algorithm-and-time-step","page":"Overview","text":"Setting Default Meaning Other choices Main source Details\nalgorithm FIRE() FIRE 2.0 minimization with the LAMMPS defaults (next table) DampedDynamics(): mathbf Mddotmathbf r = mathbf F(mathbf r) - boldsymbolGammadotmathbf r [Bitzek2006], [Guenole2020] algorithm\ntimestep CollisionTime(fraction = 1/50) Delta t = t_textcol50, with t_textcol = pisqrtM_nk_n for the lightest pair and stiffest contact (undamped Hooke) any fraction, or a fixed number [Silbert2001] timestep\n\nFIRE's parameters (§14). The first ten are the LAMMPS FIRE 2.0 defaults in [Guenole2020] (Table 1); integrator, displacement_max, damping and drag are SoftPack additions. Delta t_textstart is the step from timestep:\n\nFIRE setting Default Meaning Details\ntimestep_max 10 Delta t_textmax = 10Delta t_textstart FIRE()\ntimestep_min 0.02 Delta t_textmin = 002Delta t_textstart FIRE()\ndelay 20 N_textdelay = 20 downhill steps before the step may grow FIRE()\ntimestep_grow 1.1 Delta t leftarrow mathrmmin(11Delta t Delta t_textmax) FIRE()\ntimestep_shrink 0.5 Delta t leftarrow 05Delta t after an uphill move, if that stays ge Delta t_textmin FIRE()\nmixing_coefficient 0.25 alpha_textstart = 025 in mathbf v leftarrow (1-alpha)mathbf v + alphalvertmathbf vrverthatmathbf F FIRE()\nmixing_shrink 0.99 alpha leftarrow 099alpha on each growth step FIRE()\nuphill_max 2000 stop, unconverged, after 2000 consecutive uphill moves FIRE()\ninertia_correction true move back half a step after an uphill move (LAMMPS halfstepback) FIRE()\ndelay_initial true apply the delay at the start of the run too FIRE()\ndisplacement_max 0.1 cap one step's displacement, in reference diameters FIRE()\nintegrator :semi_implicit_euler semi-implicit (symplectic) Euler; :velocity_verlet is the option FIRE()\ndamping, drag nothing, nothing FIRE removes energy by its own velocity rules FIRE()","title":"Algorithm and time step"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#Damping-(damped-dynamics)","page":"Overview","text":"Setting Default Meaning Main source Details\ndamping ContactDamping(normal = 0.98, tangential = 0.98) gamma_n = 098 cdot 2sqrtk_n M_n, gamma_t = 098 cdot 2sqrtk_t M_t [Agnolin2007] (0.98), [Gilabert2007] (ratio form), [Somfai2005] (both directions) ContactDamping()\ntangential_mass :with_rotation 1M_t = 1m_i + 1m_j + R_i^2I_i + R_j^2I_j (M_t = M_n3 for equal disks) SoftPack choice ContactDamping()\nattractive false F_n = max(0 k_ndelta - gamma_n v_n) [Somfai2005], [LAMMPSgran] ContactDamping()\ncoulomb_limit :elastic lvert F_t^textelrvert le mu F_n^textel; tangential dashpot off while sliding [Agnolin2007] ContactDamping()\ndrag nothing no Stokes drag (a = 0 in boldsymbolGamma = amathbf M + bmathbf K_0) SoftPack choice drag\nintegrator :centered_velocity_verlet the dashpot uses the end-of-step velocity mathbf v_n+1 (implicit); the options are :lagged_velocity_verlet (mathbf v_n+12) and :semi_implicit_euler (mathbf v_n) SoftPack choice integrator\n\nWith these defaults and Hookean contacts the damping is exactly stiffness-proportional, boldsymbolGamma = bmathbf K_0 with b = 2zetaomega_c, so every underdamped mode decays at beta_ell = bomega_ell^22 (§8).\n\nThe options' own defaults: ViscousRate(normal = 0.5, tangential = 0.5), rates r = 05tau with gamma = m_texteffr [Santos2024]; RelativeVelocityDamping(rate = 0.5), mathbf F_ij = -gamma(mathbf v_i - mathbf v_j) (the CD model of [Vagberg2014]); StokesDrag(velocity = rate), mathbf F = -a_v mmathbf v, with no default rate (§8).","title":"Damping (damped dynamics)"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#Rattlers","page":"Overview","text":"Setting Default Meaning Other choices Main source Details\nrattlers ContactCount(cone_test = true) repeatedly remove particles with fewer than D + 1 contacts (frictionless) or fewer than 2 (frictional); with the cone test, also remove two-contact particles that cannot be in balance TwoContact(), ForceFloor(floor = 1e-3), Unresolved(floor = :tolerance), nothing, any function [Xiong2019], [Gilabert2007] rattlers\n\nThe options' sources: TwoContact [Ishima2023], ForceFloor [Shundyak2007], Unresolved [Agnolin2007] (§9).","title":"Rattlers"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#Reproducibility-and-outputs","page":"Overview","text":"Setting Default Meaning Other choices Main source Details\nseed 1 random numbers from StableRNG(1), the same stream on every machine and Julia version any integer 0 le textseed le 2^63 - 1 (typemax(Int)) SoftPack choice (value); [StableRNGs], [JuliaRandom] (generator) seed\nfile format (write_packing) HDF5 one binary file, read natively by Julia, Python and MATLAB CSV [HDF5jl], [h5py], [MATLABh5read] path\nwrite_packing rattlers_file false the file holds the packing without rattlers only true: a second file with every particle SoftPack choice rattlers_file\nwrite_packing compression nothing uncompressed, so datasets can be memory-mapped :gzip (level 4) [HDF5jl], [h5py] compression\nwrite_packing overwrite false an existing file stops the write with an error true: replace the existing file SoftPack choice overwrite\nsnapshots, movie nothing, nothing nothing recorded during the run Snapshots(every = interval), Movie(path; every = interval) SoftPack choice snapshots, movie","title":"Reproducibility and outputs"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/#References","page":"Overview","text":"[OHern2003] Corey S. O’Hern, Leonardo E. Silbert, Andrea J. Liu, Sidney R. Nagel (2003). Jamming at zero temperature and zero applied stress: The epitome of disorder. Physical Review E 68, 011306. doi:10.1103/physreve.68.011306.\n[Somfai2005] Ellák Somfai, Jean-Noël Roux, Jacco H. Snoeijer, Martin van Hecke, Wim van Saarloos (2005). Elastic wave propagation in confined granular systems. Physical Review E 72, 021301. doi:10.1103/physreve.72.021301.\n[LAMMPSset] LAMMPS developers. LAMMPS documentation: set command. https://docs.lammps.org/set.html. Accessed 2026-11-18.\n[Agnolin2007] Ivana Agnolin, Jean-Noël Roux (2007). Internal states of model isotropic granular packings. I. Assembling process, geometry, and contact networks. Physical Review E 76, 061302. doi:10.1103/physreve.76.061302.\n[Silbert2001] Leonardo E. Silbert, Deniz Ertaş, Gary S. Grest, Thomas C. Halsey, Dov Levine, Steven J. Plimpton (2001). Granular flow down an inclined plane: Bagnold scaling and rheology. Physical Review E 64, 051302. doi:10.1103/physreve.64.051302.\n[Santos2024] A. P. Santos, Ishan Srivastava, Leonardo E. Silbert, Jeremy B. Lechman, Gary S. Grest (2024). Protocol-dependent frictional granular jamming simulations: cyclical, compression, and expansion. Frontiers in Soft Matter 3, 1326756. doi:10.3389/frsfm.2023.1326756.\n[Shundyak2007] Kostya Shundyak, Martin van Hecke, Wim van Saarloos (2007). Force mobilization and generalized isostaticity in jammed packings of frictional grains. Physical Review E 75, 010301. doi:10.1103/physreve.75.010301.\n[Gilabert2007] F. A. Gilabert, J.-N. Roux, A. Castellanos (2007). Computer simulation of model cohesive powders: Influence of assembling procedure and contact laws on low consolidation states. Physical Review E 75, 011303. doi:10.1103/physreve.75.011303.\n[LAMMPSreadrestart] LAMMPS developers. LAMMPS documentation: read_restart command. https://docs.lammps.org/read_restart.html. Accessed 2026-11-19.\n[LAMMPSrestart] LAMMPS developers. LAMMPS documentation: restart command. https://docs.lammps.org/restart.html. Accessed 2026-11-19.\n[Xiong2019] Fansheng Xiong, Philip Wang, Abram H. Clark, Thibault Bertrand, Nicholas T. Ouellette, Mark D. Shattuck, Corey S. O’Hern (2019). Comparison of shear and compression jammed packings of frictional disks. Granular Matter 21, 109. doi:10.1007/s10035-019-0964-9.\n[Bitzek2006] Erik Bitzek, Pekka Koskinen, Franz Gähler, Michael Moseler, Peter Gumbsch (2006). Structural Relaxation Made Simple. Physical Review Letters 97, 170201. doi:10.1103/physrevlett.97.170201.\n[Guenole2020] Julien Guénolé, Wolfram G. Nöhring, Aviral Vaid, Frédéric Houllé, Zhuocheng Xie, Aruna Prakash, Erik Bitzek (2020). Assessment and optimization of the fast inertial relaxation engine (fire) for energy minimization in atomistic simulations and its implementation in lammps. Computational Materials Science 175, 109584. doi:10.1016/j.commatsci.2020.109584.\n[LAMMPSgran] LAMMPS developers. LAMMPS documentation: pair_style gran/hooke, gran/hooke/history, gran/hertz/history. https://docs.lammps.org/pair_gran.html. Accessed 2026-11-18.\n[Vagberg2014] Daniel Vågberg, Peter Olsson, S. Teitel (2014). Dissipation and Rheology of Sheared Soft-Core Frictionless Disks Below Jamming. Physical Review Letters 112, 208303. doi:10.1103/PhysRevLett.112.208303. arXiv:1311.4902.\n[Ishima2023] Daisuke Ishima, Kuniyasu Saitoh, Michio Otsuki, Hisao Hayakawa (2023). Theory of rigidity and numerical analysis of density of states of two-dimensional amorphous solids with dispersed frictional grains in the linear response regime. Physical Review E 107, 054902. doi:10.1103/physreve.107.054902.\n[StableRNGs] JuliaRandom. StableRNGs.jl. https://github.com/JuliaRandom/StableRNGs.jl. Accessed 2026-11-18.\n[JuliaRandom] The Julia Project. Random Numbers, The Julia Language manual (Reproducibility section). https://docs.julialang.org/en/v1/stdlib/Random/. Accessed 2026-11-18.\n[HDF5jl] JuliaIO. HDF5.jl documentation. https://juliaio.github.io/HDF5.jl/stable/. Accessed 2026-11-18.\n[h5py] The h5py developers. Datasets, h5py documentation. https://docs.h5py.org/en/stable/high/dataset.html. Accessed 2026-11-18.\n[MATLABh5read] The MathWorks. h5read: Read data from HDF5 dataset, MATLAB documentation. https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/h5read.html. Accessed 2026-11-18.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/LeidenAcceleration/#LeidenAcceleration()","page":"LeidenAcceleration()","text":"Up: convergence\n\nThe test used by the Leiden group [Somfai2005], [Shundyak2007]: every particle's acceleration must be below a tolerance in reduced units,\n\nmax_i a_i  varepsilon  frack  d_textrefm_textref\n\nwith k the contact stiffness and d_textref, m_textref the reference diameter and mass. The right-hand side is the acceleration a contact force of k d_textref would give the reference mass, so varepsilon is again relative.\n\nWith friction the rotational part counts too: the angular acceleration mathbf T_iI_i, times d_textref so that it carries the same units, must be below the same threshold. The paper states the translational rule only; including the rotation is a SoftPack choice, since a particle spinning in place is not at rest.\n\nLeidenAcceleration() replaces the RelativeForce test for the force balance; the pressure band is asked separately, as for every test.","title":"LeidenAcceleration()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/LeidenAcceleration/#Options","page":"LeidenAcceleration()","text":"Option Default Meaning\ntolerance 1e-10 varepsilon, relative to the contact acceleration","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/LeidenAcceleration/#Example","page":"LeidenAcceleration()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    convergence = LeidenAcceleration(\n        tolerance = 1e-10,  # acceleration in reduced units\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.converged","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/LeidenAcceleration/#References","page":"LeidenAcceleration()","text":"[Somfai2005] Ellák Somfai, Jean-Noël Roux, Jacco H. Snoeijer, Martin van Hecke, Wim van Saarloos (2005). Elastic wave propagation in confined granular systems. Physical Review E 72, 021301. doi:10.1103/physreve.72.021301.\n[Shundyak2007] Kostya Shundyak, Martin van Hecke, Wim van Saarloos (2007). Force mobilization and generalized isostaticity in jammed packings of frictional grains. Physical Review E 75, 010301. doi:10.1103/physreve.75.010301.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/boundary/Walls/#Walls()","page":"Walls()","text":"Up: boundary\n\nRigid walls on every side of the box [LAMMPSgran]. A particle that reaches a wall is pushed back by the same contact law, with the wall as an infinite-mass partner; the contact's branch vector is the distance from the particle's center to the wall, and its lever arm is the particle radius.\n\nWalls(friction = true) is the default for the type: wall contacts then carry a tangential spring and a Coulomb cap like particle contacts, using the same friction model. Walls(friction = false) keeps the normal force only, which is the cleaner choice when wall friction would confuse a measurement of the bulk.\n\nThe pressure includes the wall virial,\n\nP = frac1D Vleft(sum_textpairs f_n  l + sum_textwalls f_n  hright)\n\nand the walls move with the box during compression, so the container shrinks with the packing.\n\nCounting a wall contact like a pair contact in the coordination number is provisional: the design leaves that open for Walls.","title":"Walls()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/boundary/Walls/#Options","page":"Walls()","text":"Option Default Meaning\nfriction true whether wall contacts carry the tangential model","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/boundary/Walls/#Example","page":"Walls()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    boundary = Walls(\n        friction = false,  # normal wall contacts only\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.packing_fraction","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/boundary/Walls/#References","page":"Walls()","text":"[LAMMPSgran] LAMMPS developers. LAMMPS documentation: pair_style gran/hooke, gran/hooke/history, gran/hertz/history. https://docs.lammps.org/pair_gran.html. Accessed 2026-11-18.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/snapshots/Snapshots/#Snapshots()","page":"Snapshots()","text":"Up: snapshots\n\nSnapshots are a SoftPack choice. Take a snapshot every every steps (or every wall-clock period). The file then holds a snapshots/positions dataset with one column per snapshot, so the k-th frame is snapshots/positions[:, k].\n\nSnapshots are cheap: each one is D N numbers, so a run with 200 snapshots of 256 disks stores 100,000 numbers, well under a megabyte. They are the right choice when the intermediate states matter for analysis but a movie does not.","title":"Snapshots()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/snapshots/Snapshots/#Options","page":"Snapshots()","text":"Option Default Meaning\nevery required steps (an integer) or a wall-clock period between snapshots","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/snapshots/Snapshots/#Example","page":"Snapshots()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    snapshots = Snapshots(\n        every = 5_000,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\nwrite_packing(\"packing.h5\", packing)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/#particles_size_distribution","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nparticles_size_distribution chooses the law that the particle diameters are drawn from. The default is a 50:50 mixture of two diameters in the ratio 1.4, the mixture that prevents crystallization in 2D jamming studies [OHern2003]. Each choice fixes the length unit through its own defaults: Bidisperse() makes the small diameter 1, UniformPolydisperse() makes the mean diameter 1 [Somfai2005], and Monodisperse() makes its one diameter 1. Every output stores each particle's diameter.","title":"particles_size_distribution"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/#The-call-with-the-default-written-out","page":"Overview","text":"pack(                                           # run the packing\n    particles_size_distribution = Bidisperse()  # two-diameter mixture (default)\n)                                               # end of pack call\n\npack(...) — runs the packing.\nparticles_size_distribution = Bidisperse() (default) — a 50:50 mixture of two diameters in the ratio 1.4.\nFor details, see Bidisperse().","title":"The call with the default written out"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Draws diameters from Length unit \nBidisperse() two diameters, large over small 1.4, half of each by number the small diameter is 1 default\nUniformPolydisperse() a uniform law between 0.8 and 1.2 times the mean the mean diameter is 1 \nMonodisperse() one diameter for every particle that diameter is 1 ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/#Related-keywords","page":"Overview","text":"particles_diameters: give the diameters themselves instead of drawing them from a distribution.\nparticles_count: the number of particles N.\nUnits and length unit: how the length unit sets every other unit.","title":"Related keywords"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/#Theory","page":"Overview","text":"For now these sections are on the wiki.\n\nParticle size distribution and length unit: the whole wiki page for this keyword.\nModel: the diameters, each law and the packing fraction of a mixture.\nDerivations: the moments of each law and how a change of length unit changes the numbers.","title":"Theory"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/#Example","page":"Overview","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    particles_size_distribution = Bidisperse(\n        diameter_small = 1.0,\n        diameter_ratio = 1.4,\n        fraction_small = 0.5,\n    ),\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.diameters","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/#References","page":"Overview","text":"[OHern2003] Corey S. O’Hern, Leonardo E. Silbert, Andrea J. Liu, Sidney R. Nagel (2003). Jamming at zero temperature and zero applied stress: The epitome of disorder. Physical Review E 68, 011306. doi:10.1103/physreve.68.011306.\n[Somfai2005] Ellák Somfai, Jean-Noël Roux, Jacco H. Snoeijer, Martin van Hecke, Wim van Saarloos (2005). Elastic wave propagation in confined granular systems. Physical Review E 72, 021301. doi:10.1103/physreve.72.021301.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/restart_file/#restart_file","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nWrite the full run state to a file during the run [LAMMPSrestart], so a job that is killed can be continued from its last save instead of from the start. The file holds everything the restart needs: positions, velocities, rotations and spins, the contact history, the compression state, the FIRE counters, the settings and the provenance.\n\nNothing is saved by default; a restart file has to be asked for.\n\nDefault: nothing","title":"restart_file"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/restart_file/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nnothing default\nRestartFile() ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/restart_file/#Example","page":"Overview","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    restart_file = RestartFile(\n        \"run.h5\",\n        every = 25_000,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/restart_file/#References","page":"Overview","text":"[LAMMPSrestart] LAMMPS developers. LAMMPS documentation: restart command. https://docs.lammps.org/restart.html. Accessed 2026-11-19.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/friction/#friction","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nFriction is the tangential force at a contact. With friction on, two touching particles also carry a tangential spring: the tangential displacement since the contact was made is stored, the spring pulls back along it, and the force is capped by Coulomb's condition\n\nf_t le mu f_n\n\nwith mu the friction coefficient [Somfai2005], [Santos2024]. A contact whose tangential force sits on the cap is sliding; the strain it has stored then stops growing. The stored displacement is dropped when the contact breaks.\n\nFriction is a model with history, so it needs the contact table to carry one tangential spring per pair, and the particles to carry rotation: one angle per particle in 2D and a rotation vector in 3D. It is also what makes packings marginal: at the same pressure a frictional packing is looser and its coordination number falls toward the isostatic value z_textiso = D + 1, which is 3 for disks and 4 for spheres, instead of the frictionless 2D.\n\nDefault: nothing (frictionless)","title":"friction"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/friction/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default Tangential stiffness\nnothing default no tangential force\nCundallStrack()  fixed ratio to the normal one\nMindlin()  Hertz-Mindlin, growing with the overlap; needs Hertzian()","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/friction/#Measured-effect","page":"Overview","text":"At P = 10^-4, N = 256, 2D, FIRE: phi = 08160 at mu = 01, 07927 at mu = 03 and 07825 at mu = 05, against 08387 frictionless.","title":"Measured effect"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/friction/#Example","page":"Overview","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    friction = CundallStrack(\n        friction_coefficient = 0.3,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.packing_fraction","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/friction/#References","page":"Overview","text":"[Somfai2005] Ellák Somfai, Jean-Noël Roux, Jacco H. Snoeijer, Martin van Hecke, Wim van Saarloos (2005). Elastic wave propagation in confined granular systems. Physical Review E 72, 021301. doi:10.1103/physreve.72.021301.\n[Santos2024] A. P. Santos, Ishan Srivastava, Leonardo E. Silbert, Jeremy B. Lechman, Gary S. Grest (2024). Protocol-dependent frictional granular jamming simulations: cyclical, compression, and expansion. Frontiers in Soft Matter 3, 1326756. doi:10.3389/frsfm.2023.1326756.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/path/#path-and-format","page":"path","text":"Up: write_packing()\n\nThe path is where the file goes; format picks what is written [HDF5jl], [h5py], [MATLABh5read].\n\nChoice Default What is written\nformat = :hdf5 from .h5 or .hdf5 one HDF5 file, read natively by Julia, Python and MATLAB\nformat = :csv from .csv two text files, <base>_particles.csv and <base>_contacts.csv\n\nThe format follows the extension: .h5 and .hdf5 are HDF5, .csv is CSV, and any other extension is an error. An explicit format = :hdf5 or format = :csv overrides the extension, for a path whose extension says nothing.\n\nLater formats (XYZ, LAMMPS data) are on the Roadmap.","title":"path and format"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/path/#HDF5-layout","page":"path","text":"Group Contents\nparticles one 1-D dataset per column: id (0-based), diameter, mass, position_x …, force_x …\ncontacts first, second (0-based; second = -1 marks a wall), wall_id, overlap, normal_force, tangential_force, sliding\nstate every particle and the full dynamic state, enough for restart_packing\nsnapshots positions, one column per snapshot, when snapshots was set\n\nThe particles group holds the remaining particles after the rattler rule; with rattlers_file = true the second file holds every particle and its flag.","title":"HDF5 layout"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/path/#Example","page":"path","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(particles_count = 64, seed = 1)\nwrite_packing(\"packing.csv\", packing)  # CSV from the extension","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/path/#References","page":"path","text":"[HDF5jl] JuliaIO. HDF5.jl documentation. https://juliaio.github.io/HDF5.jl/stable/. Accessed 2026-11-18.\n[h5py] The h5py developers. Datasets, h5py documentation. https://docs.h5py.org/en/stable/high/dataset.html. Accessed 2026-11-18.\n[MATLABh5read] The MathWorks. h5read: Read data from HDF5 dataset, MATLAB documentation. https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/h5read.html. Accessed 2026-11-18.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/restart_file/RestartFile/#RestartFile()","page":"RestartFile()","text":"Up: restart_file\n\nWrite the run state to path every every steps [LAMMPSrestart]. every can also be a Dates period, in which case the save happens on wall-clock time.\n\nThe write is atomic: the state goes to a temporary file in the same directory and is renamed over the path, so a job killed during a save leaves the previous file intact. A save costs one HDF5 write of the state arrays, which for the default run is a few milliseconds; a schedule is therefore given as a step interval rather than a save on every step.\n\nA restart file is the same format as the main file write_packing writes, so a packing saved at the end of a run can be continued too; the difference is only when the file is written.","title":"RestartFile()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/restart_file/RestartFile/#Options","page":"RestartFile()","text":"Option Default Meaning\npath required where to write the state\nevery required steps (an integer) or a wall-clock period between saves","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/restart_file/RestartFile/#Example","page":"RestartFile()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    restart_file = RestartFile(\n        \"run.h5\",\n        every = 10_000,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/restart_file/RestartFile/#References","page":"RestartFile()","text":"[LAMMPSrestart] LAMMPS developers. LAMMPS documentation: restart command. https://docs.lammps.org/restart.html. Accessed 2026-11-19.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/damping/#damping","page":"Overview","text":"Up: DampedDynamics\n\nThe contact dashpots [Agnolin2007], [Santos2024]. A dashpot removes energy in proportion to the relative velocity at a contact; SoftPack offers three ways to set its coefficient, all of them written as a damping ratio or a rate so that a run keeps its meaning when the stiffness changes.\n\nChoice Default Coefficient\nContactDamping() default gamma = 2zetasqrtk M, proportional to the contact time\nViscousRate()  gamma = M r, a fixed rate per unit mass\nRelativeVelocityDamping()  gamma = M r on the full relative velocity, one term for both directions\n\nDamping can also be given to FIRE, where it acts as extra dissipation on top of the velocity mixing.\n\nAll three take attractive and coulomb_limit. attractive = false (the default) lets the dashpot only push apart, so it never holds two particles together; attractive = true lets it act in both directions, which can help with a saddle but can also pair particles that should separate. coulomb_limit = :elastic caps the tangential dashpot by the elastic normal force; the alternative :total is designed but not implemented.","title":"damping"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/damping/#Example","page":"Overview","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    algorithm = DampedDynamics(\n        damping = ContactDamping(\n            normal = 0.98,\n            tangential = 0.98,\n        ),\n    ),\n    particles_count = 64,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.converged","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/damping/#References","page":"Overview","text":"[Agnolin2007] Ivana Agnolin, Jean-Noël Roux (2007). Internal states of model isotropic granular packings. I. Assembling process, geometry, and contact networks. Physical Review E 76, 061302. doi:10.1103/physreve.76.061302.\n[Santos2024] A. P. Santos, Ishan Srivastava, Leonardo E. Silbert, Jeremy B. Lechman, Gary S. Grest (2024). Protocol-dependent frictional granular jamming simulations: cyclical, compression, and expansion. Frontiers in Soft Matter 3, 1326756. doi:10.3389/frsfm.2023.1326756.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/timestep/#timestep","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nThe starting time step [Silbert2001], [OSullivan2004]. FIRE starts at it and adapts it as it goes; damped dynamics keeps it fixed for the whole run. It is chosen from the shortest contact time of the lightest pair, so that the fastest oscillation in the packing is resolved.\n\nThe default rule is CollisionTime(), which scales the elastic contact time. A number is also accepted; it is interpreted in units of sqrtm_mathrmmink.\n\nDefault: CollisionTime()","title":"timestep"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/timestep/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nCollisionTime() default\na number ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/timestep/#Example","page":"Overview","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    timestep = 0.02,  # a fixed step, in units of sqrt(m_min/k)\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.timestep_start","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/timestep/#References","page":"Overview","text":"[Silbert2001] Leonardo E. Silbert, Deniz Ertaş, Gary S. Grest, Thomas C. Halsey, Dov Levine, Steven J. Plimpton (2001). Granular flow down an inclined plane: Bagnold scaling and rheology. Physical Review E 64, 051302. doi:10.1103/physreve.64.051302.\n[OSullivan2004] Catherine O'Sullivan, Jonathan D. Bray (2004). Selecting a suitable time step for discrete element simulations that use the central difference time integration scheme. Engineering Computations 21, 278–303. doi:10.1108/02644400410519794.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/target/Pressure/#Pressure()","page":"Pressure()","text":"Up: target\n\nRun the compression until the measured pressure reaches pressure [Agnolin2007]. The pressure of a configuration is\n\nP = frac1D Vleft(sum_textpairs f_n  l + sum_textwalls f_n  hright)\n\nwith l the branch vector of a pair contact, h the distance from a particle's center to the wall it touches, V = L^D the box volume and D the dimension. The sum runs in index order, so the same configuration always gives the same pressure to the last bit.\n\nThe run accepts a compression step when P - P_texttarget le pressure_tolerance times P_texttarget; the default tolerance is 10^-4, so the pressure lands within one part in ten thousand.\n\nBecause a soft packing is compressed, the packing fraction depends on the pressure you ask for. In 2D at N = 256 (mean of three seeds, with z_textiso = 3992 the finite-size isostatic value used in benchmarks/results.md):\n\nP phi z - z_textiso\n10^-2 0.8626 0.5249\n10^-3 0.8423 0.1419\n10^-4 0.8383 0.0325\n10^-5 0.8380 0.0027","title":"Pressure()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/target/Pressure/#Why-it-is-the-default","page":"Pressure()","text":"The default P = 10^-4 sits where the backbone is almost isostatic and the packing is still cheap to reach: the default run takes 0.7 s.","title":"Why it is the default"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/target/Pressure/#Options","page":"Pressure()","text":"Option Default Meaning\npressure 1e-4 the target pressure, in reduced units\n\nBoth Pressure() and Pressure(1e-3) work.","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/target/Pressure/#Example","page":"Pressure()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    target = Pressure(1e-4),  # compress until the contact pressure is 1e-4\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.pressure","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/target/Pressure/#References","page":"Pressure()","text":"[Agnolin2007] Ivana Agnolin, Jean-Noël Roux (2007). Internal states of model isotropic granular packings. I. Assembling process, geometry, and contact networks. Physical Review E 76, 061302. doi:10.1103/physreve.76.061302.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/custom_rule/#Your-own-rule-function","page":"Your own rule function","text":"Up: rattlers\n\nrattlers also accepts a function. It is called with the finished Packing and must return one value per particle, truthy for a rattler:\n\nA BitVector or a Vector{Bool} of length N is the natural return; anything that BitVector(...) accepts works. The function sees the packing's contacts table, positions, forces, diameters, masses, box_side, settings and the rest of the Packing fields, so it can use the contact network, the forces, or both.\n\nThe function is applied once, as you wrote it: it is your job to repeat or cascade if the rule needs it. To test a rule on a finished packing without repacking, use find_rattlers(packing, rule).","title":"Your own rule function"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/custom_rule/#Example","page":"Your own rule function","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    rattlers = p -> p.forces[1, :] .== 0,  # x force per particle\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\ncount(packing.rattlers)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/#rattlers","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nA rattler is a particle that carries no load [Xiong2019], [Gilabert2007]: it touches too few neighbors, or its contacts are arranged so that no combination of forces inside the friction cones can hold it. Rattlers are part of the packing but not part of its backbone, and leaving them in distorts the coordination number, so SoftPack marks them and reports the packing fraction and coordination of the remaining particles.\n\nEvery rule in this section works the same way: build the effective contact network, remove the particles that do not have enough contacts, rebuild without them, and repeat until nothing changes. Removing a particle takes a contact away from its neighbors, which can push them below the threshold in turn, so the removal cascades. The flags of every particle are written to the file even when the remaining ones are what the main datasets hold.\n\nnothing removes no particle at all, which is useful for debugging a rule or for keeping a dilute packing intact.\n\nDefault: ContactCount()","title":"rattlers"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default Rule\nContactCount() default fewer than D+1 contacts frictionless, fewer than 2 with friction\nTwoContact()  two or fewer contacts\nForceFloor()  contacts below a fraction of the mean force do not count\nUnresolved()  contacts below the convergence tolerance do not count\nYour own rule function  any function of the packing\nnothing  remove none\nfind_rattlers()  apply any rule to a finished packing","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/#Example","page":"Overview","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    rattlers = ContactCount(\n        cone_test = true,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\ncount(packing.rattlers)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/#References","page":"Overview","text":"[Xiong2019] Fansheng Xiong, Philip Wang, Abram H. Clark, Thibault Bertrand, Nicholas T. Ouellette, Mark D. Shattuck, Corey S. O’Hern (2019). Comparison of shear and compression jammed packings of frictional disks. Granular Matter 21, 109. doi:10.1007/s10035-019-0964-9.\n[Gilabert2007] F. A. Gilabert, J.-N. Roux, A. Castellanos (2007). Computer simulation of model cohesive powders: Influence of assembling procedure and contact laws on low consolidation states. Physical Review E 75, 011303. doi:10.1103/physreve.75.011303.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_diameters/#particles_diameters","page":"particles_diameters","text":"Up: Available options\n\nGive the diameters themselves instead of drawing them from particles_size_distribution. By default no list is given and the diameters are drawn from the distribution.","title":"particles_diameters"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_diameters/#The-call-with-the-default-written-out","page":"particles_diameters","text":"pack(                                # run the packing\n    particles_diameters = nothing,   # draw diameters from the size distribution (default)\n)                                    # end of pack call\n\npack(...) — runs the packing.\nparticles_diameters = nothing (default) — the diameters are drawn from particles_size_distribution.","title":"The call with the default written out"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_diameters/#Giving-the-diameters","page":"particles_diameters","text":"pack(                                            # run the packing with given diameters\n    particles_diameters = [1.0, 1.4, 1.0, 1.4]   # exactly these diameters, one per particle; particles_count is then 4\n)                                                # end of pack call\n\nparticles_diameters = [1.0, 1.4, 1.0, 1.4] — exactly these diameters, one per particle.\nparticles_count is then the number of diameters, here 4.\nGive a distribution or the diameters, not both.\nThe length unit is then whatever unit the given diameters are in.\nWith k = 1 the pressure unit follows from it.\nThe reference diameter d_textref is the mean of the given diameters: SoftPack choice, because no cited paper defines a scale for an arbitrary list and the mean is the Leiden convention for polydisperse packings.","title":"Giving the diameters"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_diameters/#Why-this-default","page":"particles_diameters","text":"SoftPack choice: an exact list is how a packing is matched to an experiment or to another code's particles.","title":"Why this default"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_diameters/#Theory","page":"particles_diameters","text":"For now these sections are on the wiki.\n\nGiven diameter list\nGiven diameters","title":"Theory"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_diameters/#Example","page":"particles_diameters","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    particles_diameters = [1.0, 1.4, 1.0, 1.4],  # one diameter per particle\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.diameters","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/seed/#seed","page":"seed","text":"Up: Available options\n\nThe seed of the random number generator [StableRNGs], [JuliaRandom]. SoftPack draws the initial placement from one StableRNG stream, and nothing else in the run is random: the compression is deterministic once the start is fixed. The same seed therefore gives the same packing to the last bit, on Julia 1.10, 1.12 and 1.13, because every reduction in the trajectory runs in a fixed order.\n\nA seed must lie between 0 and typemax(Int); anything outside raises an error rather than wrapping, because a silent modulo would map two seeds to the same packing. Use a different seed to get an independent member of the ensemble; use the same seed to reproduce a run or to compare two settings on the same start.\n\nDefault: 1","title":"seed"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/seed/#Options","page":"seed","text":"Value Meaning\nany integer the seed of the single StableRNG stream\n\nThe result records the seed and the generator, so a packing file says how to reproduce it.","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/seed/#Example","page":"seed","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    seed = 7,  # independent member of the ensemble\n    particles_count = 256,\n)\npacking.packing_fraction","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/seed/#References","page":"seed","text":"[StableRNGs] JuliaRandom. StableRNGs.jl. https://github.com/JuliaRandom/StableRNGs.jl. Accessed 2026-11-18.\n[JuliaRandom] The Julia Project. Random Numbers, The Julia Language manual (Reproducibility section). https://docs.julialang.org/en/v1/stdlib/Random/. Accessed 2026-11-18.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/ForceFloor/#ForceFloor()","page":"ForceFloor()","text":"Up: rattlers\n\nA contact whose normal force is below a fraction of the mean contact force does not count as a contact [Shundyak2007]:\n\nf_n  textfloor times langle f_n rangle\n\nThe particle then needs D + 1 effective contacts without friction, or two with friction, exactly like ContactCount(), and the removal cascades as usual. With the default floor 10^-3, contacts that carry a thousandth of the mean force are treated as broken, so a particle held only by such contacts is removed.\n\nThe rule is a compromise: ContactCount() ignores forces and can keep a particle that is barely touching, while Unresolved() uses the convergence tolerance and can be too strict at a loose tolerance. ForceFloor() puts the threshold where you set it.","title":"ForceFloor()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/ForceFloor/#Options","page":"ForceFloor()","text":"Option Default Meaning\nfloor 1e-3 the force threshold, relative to the mean contact force\ncone_test true apply the friction-cone test to two-contact particles","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/ForceFloor/#Example","page":"ForceFloor()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    rattlers = ForceFloor(\n        floor = 1e-3,\n        cone_test = true,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\ncount(packing.rattlers)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/ForceFloor/#References","page":"ForceFloor()","text":"[Shundyak2007] Kostya Shundyak, Martin van Hecke, Wim van Saarloos (2007). Force mobilization and generalized isostaticity in jammed packings of frictional grains. Physical Review E 75, 010301. doi:10.1103/physreve.75.010301.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"benchmarks/friction/#Friction-(Silbert-2010)","page":"Friction (Silbert 2010)","text":"Up: Benchmarks\n\n[Silbert2010] compressed soft spheres towards jamming for a range of friction coefficients and extrapolated the jamming packing fraction varphi_c^mu and coordination z_c^mu at P to 0. The packing fraction falls from random close packing, varphi_textrcp approx 064, to random loose packing, varphi_textrlp approx 055, as friction grows; the coordination falls from about 6 to 4. The 2D bidisperse row of the same table runs from 0843 to 0767, with z from 396 to 297.","title":"Friction (Silbert 2010)"},{"category":"section","location":"benchmarks/friction/#Figure","page":"Friction (Silbert 2010)","text":"(Image: Friction sweep against Silbert 2010)\n\nPacking fraction (top) and coordination (bottom) against mu, for 2D bidisperse disks (left) and 3D monodisperse spheres (right). Filled circles are SoftPack at P = 10^-4; open squares are the extrapolated values of [Silbert2010]; the dotted lines in the 2D panel are the compression-protocol limits of [Xiong2019].","title":"Figure"},{"category":"section","location":"benchmarks/friction/#Numbers","page":"Friction (Silbert 2010)","text":"D mu varphi SoftPack varphi Silbert z SoftPack z Silbert\n2 0 0.8413 0.843 4.042 3.96\n2 0.01 0.8364 0.842 3.979 3.85\n2 0.1 0.8233 0.836 3.702 3.73\n2 0.2 0.8032 0.827 3.492 3.59\n2 0.5 0.7926 0.801 3.217 3.15\n2 1 0.7796 0.779 3.104 2.91\n3 0 0.6369 0.639 6.111 5.96\n3 0.1 0.6013 0.614 5.349 5.17\n3 0.2 0.5882 0.595 4.913 4.60\n3 0.5 0.5718 0.574 4.407 4.22\n3 1 0.5615 0.556 4.210 3.98","title":"Numbers"},{"category":"section","location":"benchmarks/friction/#What-agrees-and-what-does-not","page":"Friction (Silbert 2010)","text":"The 3D monodisperse row, which is the closest match to Silbert's system, agrees within 0013 in packing fraction and 031 in coordination over the full range. The endpoints of the 2D row also agree: frictionless SoftPack gives 08413 against 0843, and mu = 1 gives 07796 against 0779.\n\nIn 2D at mu = 01 to 05 SoftPack sits up to 0024 below Silbert's extrapolated values. This is a protocol difference, not a failed check. Silbert approaches jamming by decreasing the packing fraction from a dense state; SoftPack compresses from a dilute start. [Xiong2019], who use isotropic compression, report varphi_J from about 0835 to 0765 and z_J from 4 to 3, with a crossover near mu_c = 01; the SoftPack 2D curve lies inside those limits and follows the compression protocol. The 2D coordination at mu = 1 is 310 against Silbert's 291, and the frictionless anchors show the same small positive offset (+008 in 2D, +015 in 3D), so the offset is finite-size, not a friction-model error.","title":"What agrees and what does not"},{"category":"section","location":"benchmarks/friction/#Reproduce","page":"Friction (Silbert 2010)","text":"The run script, the raw CSV and the analysis script are in benchmarks/paper_figures.","title":"Reproduce"},{"category":"section","location":"benchmarks/friction/#References","page":"Friction (Silbert 2010)","text":"[Silbert2010] Leonardo E. Silbert (2010). Jamming of frictional spheres and random loose packing. Soft Matter 6, 2918–2924. doi:10.1039/c001973a.\n[Xiong2019] Fansheng Xiong, Philip Wang, Abram H. Clark, Thibault Bertrand, Nicholas T. Ouellette, Mark D. Shattuck, Corey S. O’Hern (2019). Comparison of shear and compression jammed packings of frictional disks. Granular Matter 21, 109. doi:10.1007/s10035-019-0964-9.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/initial_state/Dilute/#Dilute()","page":"Dilute()","text":"Up: initial_state\n\nRandom sequential addition at rest [Agnolin2007]. The particles are placed one at a time, largest first, at uniformly random positions; a trial that overlaps a particle already placed is rejected and a new one is drawn. The placement stops at the requested packing fraction, and the box is sized to hold it:\n\nL_0 = left(fracsum_i pi d_i^24phi_0right)^12 quad text(2D) qquad L_0 = left(fracsum_i pi d_i^36phi_0right)^13 quad text(3D)\n\nNo particle moves during the placement, so the state has no contacts and zero energy, and the compression starts from a well-defined dilute configuration.","title":"Dilute()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/initial_state/Dilute/#Why-it-is-the-default","page":"Dilute()","text":"The default phi_0 = 025 is low enough that the placement never jams for the default mixture: the largest particle is the one that limits the trials, and at a quarter packing fraction there is room for it.","title":"Why it is the default"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/initial_state/Dilute/#Options","page":"Dilute()","text":"Option Default Meaning\npacking_fraction 0.25 phi_0 of the placement, between 0 and 1","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/initial_state/Dilute/#Example","page":"Dilute()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    initial_state = Dilute(\n        packing_fraction = 0.25,  # random start below jamming\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.packing_fraction","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/initial_state/Dilute/#References","page":"Dilute()","text":"[Agnolin2007] Ivana Agnolin, Jean-Noël Roux (2007). Internal states of model isotropic granular packings. I. Assembling process, geometry, and contact networks. Physical Review E 76, 061302. doi:10.1103/physreve.76.061302.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/coverage/#Tested-combinations","page":"Tested combinations","text":"Up: Reference\n\nPkg.test() checks the physics on small cases. On top of it, benchmarks/coverage.jl runs every exported entry point and every legal combination of the pack() options at a small size, and writes one line per attempt to coverage.csv. The last run made 267 attempts: 255 succeeded and 12 were inputs that must be refused, with no legal combination failing.\n\nThe sweep covers:\n\nevery exported function: pack, the Packing accessors, find_rattlers with every rule and with a user function, and write_packing in both formats;\nevery setting on its own away from the default;\ndimension, contact law, friction law, mass rule and boundary, crossed;\nFIRE and the three damped-dynamics integrators, crossed with every damping model, every drag model and friction on and off;\nevery convergence test with every algorithm;\nevery rattler rule with and without friction;\nHDF5 and CSV output with every compression and rattlers-file choice;\nrestart files, snapshots, movies, and restarts that must be refused;\nthe inputs that must raise an ArgumentError.\n\nThe sweep found three defects that the unit tests had missed: the mid-run restart file was never written (its call had a stale signature), restarts of wall systems failed on the wall tangential springs, and four exported accessors had no method for a Packing.\n\nTo rerun it:\n\njulia --project=. benchmarks/coverage.jl\n\nThe run takes about six minutes. benchmarks/coverage.md lists what each section varies and what the sweep found.\n\nPaper-level benchmarks against the O'Hern 2003 and Silbert 2010 figures are in benchmarks/paper_figures: 118 runs and three figures that reproduce the published jamming values and scaling.","title":"Tested combinations"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/#Saving-a-packing-with-write_packing()","page":"Overview","text":"Up: SoftPack\n\npack() returns a Packing in memory and writes no file. write_packing() is the separate call that writes one Packing to disk. Every option has a default, so the shortest call is the path and the packing.","title":"Saving a packing with write_packing()"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/#The-call-with-every-default-written-out","page":"Overview","text":"write_packing(                  # write one Packing to disk\n    \"my_packing.h5\",            # output path; the extension picks HDF5 or CSV\n    my_packing;                 # the Packing returned by pack(); the ; starts the keyword arguments\n    rattlers_file = false,      # write only the remaining particles (default)\n    compression   = nothing,    # leave the datasets uncompressed (default)\n    overwrite     = false       # stop with an error if a file already exists (default)\n)                               # end of write_packing call\n\nwrite_packing(...) — writes one Packing to disk, as a call separate from pack().\nFor details, see the Packing page.\n\"my_packing.h5\" — the output path; .h5 or .hdf5 writes HDF5, .csv writes CSV.\nHDF5 is the default format, read natively by Julia, Python and MATLAB [HDF5jl], [h5py], [MATLABh5read].\nFor details, see path and file format.\nmy_packing — the Packing to write.\nThe ; ends the positional arguments and starts the keyword arguments (Julia syntax).\nrattlers_file = false (default) — write only the particles that remain after the rattler rule has run.\nWith true, a second file also holds every particle with a rattler flag.\nThe second file is named after the main file: my_packing.h5 gives my_packing_rattlers.h5, in the same directory.\nFor details, see rattlers_file.\ncompression = nothing (default) — leave the datasets uncompressed, so they can be memory-mapped.\nWith :gzip, the datasets are compressed with lossless gzip at level 4.\nFor details, see compression.\noverwrite = false (default) — stop with an error if a file it would write already exists.\nWith true, the existing file is replaced.\nEach file is written to a temporary file and then renamed, so a job killed during a write leaves an existing file intact.\nFor details, see overwrite.","title":"The call with every default written out"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/#Where-next","page":"Overview","text":"path and file format: HDF5 and CSV.\nrattlers_file: the second file with every particle.\ncompression: gzip.\noverwrite: replacing an existing file, and safe writes.\npack(): creating the packing to write.","title":"Where next"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/#Example","page":"Overview","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(particles_count = 64, seed = 1)\nwrite_packing(\n    \"packing.h5\",\n    packing;\n    rattlers_file = false,\n    compression = nothing,\n    overwrite = false,\n)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/#References","page":"Overview","text":"[HDF5jl] JuliaIO. HDF5.jl documentation. https://juliaio.github.io/HDF5.jl/stable/. Accessed 2026-11-18.\n[h5py] The h5py developers. Datasets, h5py documentation. https://docs.h5py.org/en/stable/high/dataset.html. Accessed 2026-11-18.\n[MATLABh5read] The MathWorks. h5read: Read data from HDF5 dataset, MATLAB documentation. https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/h5read.html. Accessed 2026-11-18.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/failure_action/#failure_action","page":"failure_action","text":"Up: Available options\n\nWhat a run does when it stops without converging. The default is a SoftPack choice.\n\nChoice Default Behavior\n:warn default log a warning and return the packing with converged = false\n:error  throw an error instead\n\nThe default returns the packing because a run that stopped at steps_max or :uphill_max still holds a physically meaningful state, and because a batch of seeds should not die on one bad one: the results say which runs failed and the caller decides what to do.\n\n:error is the choice for a production run where a failed packing must not enter an ensemble silently.\n\nDefault: :warn","title":"failure_action"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/failure_action/#Options","page":"failure_action","text":"Choice Default\n:warn default\n:error ","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/failure_action/#Example","page":"failure_action","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    failure_action = :error,  # throw PackingError instead of warning\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/UniformPolydisperse/#UniformPolydisperse()","page":"UniformPolydisperse()","text":"Up: particles_size_distribution\n\nDiameters drawn uniformly around a mean, with the mean diameter as the unit of length. It is not the default: use it to match the Leiden frictional packings [Somfai2005], [Shundyak2007].","title":"UniformPolydisperse()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/UniformPolydisperse/#The-call-with-every-option-written-out","page":"UniformPolydisperse()","text":"pack(                                                    # run the packing\n    particles_size_distribution = UniformPolydisperse(   # diameters uniform around a mean\n        diameter_mean = 1.0,                             # mean diameter; sets the length unit (default)\n        spread        = 0.2,                             # half-width over the mean (default)\n    )                                                    # end of UniformPolydisperse call\n)                                                        # end of pack call\n\npack(...) — runs the packing.\nparticles_size_distribution = UniformPolydisperse(...) — diameters drawn uniformly between d_textmean(1-s) and d_textmean(1+s).\n\np(d) = frac12sd_textmean quad textfor  d_textmean(1-s) le d le d_textmean(1+s)\n\ndiameter_mean = 1.0 (default) — the mean diameter, which also sets the length unit: d_textref = d_textmean.\n[Somfai2005] \"Our unit of length is the average grain diameter.\"\nThe drawn diameters are not rescaled to make their sample mean exactly d_textmean (SoftPack choice), so the output stores the realized mean langle drangle.\n[Shundyak2007] \"our units are such that the mass density, the average particle diameter, and the Young's modulus of the grains are 1.\"\nspread = 0.2 (default) — the half-width over the mean, s = textttspread.\nThe polydispersity (standard deviation over mean) is ssqrt3.\nThe diameters stay positive for s  1 (SoftPack note).\n[Somfai2005] \"For the 2D simulations the spheres have polydispersity to avoid crystallization: the diameters are uniformly distributed between 0.8 and 1.2 times their average.\"","title":"The call with every option written out"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/UniformPolydisperse/#Why-this-option","page":"UniformPolydisperse()","text":"It matches the Leiden frictional packings ([Somfai2005], [Shundyak2007]).\nTheir 2D particles are spheres for the mass (see mass).","title":"Why this option"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/UniformPolydisperse/#Theory","page":"UniformPolydisperse()","text":"For now these sections are on the wiki.\n\nUniform polydisperse law\nUniform moments\nChanging the length unit","title":"Theory"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/UniformPolydisperse/#Example","page":"UniformPolydisperse()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    particles_size_distribution = UniformPolydisperse(\n        diameter_mean = 1.0,  # mean diameter; sets the length unit\n        spread = 0.2,         # diameters in [0.8, 1.2]\n    ),\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.diameters","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/particles_size_distribution/UniformPolydisperse/#References","page":"UniformPolydisperse()","text":"[Somfai2005] Ellák Somfai, Jean-Noël Roux, Jacco H. Snoeijer, Martin van Hecke, Wim van Saarloos (2005). Elastic wave propagation in confined granular systems. Physical Review E 72, 021301. doi:10.1103/physreve.72.021301.\n[Shundyak2007] Kostya Shundyak, Martin van Hecke, Wim van Saarloos (2007). Force mobilization and generalized isostaticity in jammed packings of frictional grains. Physical Review E 75, 010301. doi:10.1103/physreve.75.010301.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/centered_velocity_verlet/#:centered_velocity_verlet","page":":centered_velocity_verlet","text":"Up: integrator\n\nThe default integrator [Samiei2013]. The damping is evaluated at the midpoint of the step, which turns the velocity update into a linear system:\n\nleft(M + fracDelta t2 Gammaright) v^n+1 = left(M - fracDelta t2 Gammaright) v^n + Delta t  F^n\n\nand the matrix on the left is sparse, symmetric and positive definite, so the implementation assembles and factorizes it once per step, then solves it once per step.\n\nBecause the damping is treated implicitly, the integrator is stable for any damping ratio at the time step the contacts allow, and the damping does not lag the motion. That is why it is the default for production runs.","title":":centered_velocity_verlet"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/centered_velocity_verlet/#Options","page":":centered_velocity_verlet","text":"None; the integrator is chosen by name.","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/centered_velocity_verlet/#Example","page":":centered_velocity_verlet","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    algorithm = DampedDynamics(\n        integrator = :centered_velocity_verlet,  # the default\n    ),\n    particles_count = 64,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.converged","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/centered_velocity_verlet/#References","page":":centered_velocity_verlet","text":"[Samiei2013] K. Samiei, B. Peters, M. Bolten, A. Frommer (2013). Assessment of the potentials of implicit integration method in discrete element modelling of granular matter. Computers & Chemical Engineering 49, 183–193. doi:10.1016/j.compchemeng.2012.10.009.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/#integrator","page":"Overview","text":"Up: DampedDynamics\n\nHow the equations of motion are stepped [Samiei2013], [CodeAster]. The three integrators differ in when they evaluate the damping: the centered velocity Verlet solves the damping implicitly and is the most robust; the lagged velocity Verlet uses the previous step's relative velocities and is explicit; the semi-implicit Euler is the simplest explicit scheme.\n\nChoice Default Damping\n:centered_velocity_verlet default implicit, one sparse Cholesky solve per step\n:lagged_velocity_verlet  from the previous step's relative velocities\n:semi_implicit_euler  explicit, evaluated at the current velocities\n\nAll three are stable at the run's time step, which CollisionTime sets from the stiffest contact. Their stable limits differ, 2omega for the centered form and 2(sqrt1+zeta^2-zeta)omega for the other two, and the packer warns if the chosen step exceeds the limit of the chosen integrator.\n\nThe centered form is the default because it tolerates the largest step and does not introduce a lag in the damping. It costs a sparse Cholesky solve of M + tfracDelta t2Gamma every step; with N = 256 that is cheaper than the extra steps the explicit forms need.","title":"integrator"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/#Example","page":"Overview","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    algorithm = DampedDynamics(\n        integrator = :centered_velocity_verlet,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 64,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.converged","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/integrator/#References","page":"Overview","text":"[Samiei2013] K. Samiei, B. Peters, M. Bolten, A. Frommer (2013). Assessment of the potentials of implicit integration method in discrete element modelling of granular matter. Computers & Chemical Engineering 49, 183–193. doi:10.1016/j.compchemeng.2012.10.009.\n[CodeAster] CodeAster developers. *Algorithmes d'intégration directe de l'opérateur DYNAVIBRA / BASECALCUL='PHYS', manual R5.05.02*. <https://code-aster.org/doc/v16/manr/r5/r5.05.02.pdf>. Section 6.2, central differences. Accessed 2026-11-24.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/ContactCount/#ContactCount()","page":"ContactCount()","text":"Up: rattlers\n\nRemove every particle that does not have enough contacts to be held [Xiong2019], [Gilabert2007]. Without friction a particle needs D + 1 contacts: three in 2D and four in 3D. With friction two are enough, because a pair of contacts inside the friction cones can hold it.\n\nThe rule is applied repeatedly. Each round rebuilds the network without the particles already removed; a neighbor that drops below the threshold goes too, and the rounds stop when a round changes nothing. Removing a particle takes contacts away from its neighbors, so a packing just above its rigidity threshold can lose a chain of particles from one seed.","title":"ContactCount()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/ContactCount/#Why-it-is-the-default","page":"ContactCount()","text":"This is the default rule because it is the one that matches the isostatic argument: at low pressure the backbone should have exactly the number of contacts that rigidity requires.","title":"Why it is the default"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/ContactCount/#The-cone-test","page":"ContactCount()","text":"With friction, a particle with exactly two contacts can only be held if its two contact forces can point into the friction cones. The two contact directions enclose an angle theta, and a pair of forces inside the cones can only balance when\n\ntheta ge pi - 2arctan mu\n\nWith mu = 03 that is theta ge 146^circ: the two contacts must be nearly opposite. A two-contact particle that fails the test is removed, because no force it can transmit would hold it. cone_test = true (the default) applies it.\n\nThe cone test runs under every rule except TwoContact(), which removes two-contact particles before the test could matter. It sees only contacts above the rule's force floor, so a contact the rule has already declared broken cannot keep a particle.\n\nThe cone test is also where a bug in an earlier version emptied whole frictional packings: the direction from a particle to its contact point is -n when the particle is the first member of the pair and +n when it is the second, and using n for both mixed the angle with its complement.","title":"The cone test"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/ContactCount/#Options","page":"ContactCount()","text":"Option Default Meaning\ncone_test true apply the friction-cone test to two-contact particles","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/ContactCount/#Example","page":"ContactCount()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    rattlers = ContactCount(\n        cone_test = true,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\ncount(packing.rattlers)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/rattlers/ContactCount/#References","page":"ContactCount()","text":"[Xiong2019] Fansheng Xiong, Philip Wang, Abram H. Clark, Thibault Bertrand, Nicholas T. Ouellette, Mark D. Shattuck, Corey S. O’Hern (2019). Comparison of shear and compression jammed packings of frictional disks. Granular Matter 21, 109. doi:10.1007/s10035-019-0964-9.\n[Gilabert2007] F. A. Gilabert, J.-N. Roux, A. Castellanos (2007). Computer simulation of model cohesive powders: Influence of assembling procedure and contact laws on low consolidation states. Physical Review E 75, 011303. doi:10.1103/physreve.75.011303.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/RestartTest/#RestartTest()","page":"RestartTest()","text":"Up: convergence\n\nThe check for saddles [Agnolin2007]. It first requires the RelativeForce test at its default tolerance, then zeroes the velocities on the final packing, relaxes once more, and asks whether the kinetic energy and the unbalanced forces stay down. A packing that passes the force test but drifts as soon as it is nudged was sitting on a saddle or on a marginal contact, and the restart test catches it.\n\nIt has three options:\n\nOption Default Meaning\nwatch 2000 steps of relaxation after the velocities are zeroed\ngrowth 2.0 how much the largest unbalanced force may grow\nenergy_tolerance 1e-8 the kinetic-energy scale, as in KineticEnergy\n\nThe first part of the test is the force balance at the RelativeForce default, 10^-8.\n\nThis is the most expensive test of the five, because it runs a second short minimization for every acceptance.","title":"RestartTest()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/RestartTest/#Example","page":"RestartTest()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    convergence = RestartTest(\n        watch = 2000,        # steps after zeroing the velocities\n        growth = 2.0,        # allowed force growth\n        energy_tolerance = 1e-8,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.converged","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/RestartTest/#References","page":"RestartTest()","text":"[Agnolin2007] Ivana Agnolin, Jean-Noël Roux (2007). Internal states of model isotropic granular packings. I. Assembling process, geometry, and contact networks. Physical Review E 76, 061302. doi:10.1103/physreve.76.061302.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/rendering/#Images-and-movies","page":"Images and movies","text":"Up: Reference\n\nEvery image SoftPack writes comes from the same renderer, SoftPack.render_frame!, which draws the current state into a PPM file with no plotting library. A movie is a directory of such frames encoded by ffmpeg at the end of the run; a snapshot stores positions rather than pixels, so it can be redrawn later at any size.","title":"Images and movies"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/rendering/#What-a-frame-shows","page":"Images and movies","text":"Disks in 2D and spheres seen from above in 3D, colored by diameter: the small population in one color, the large one in another.\nIn 3D the far particles are drawn first, smaller and blended toward white, so the depth reads without lighting or transparency.\nIn a periodic box a particle that crosses the frame is drawn again on the opposite side, so the packing looks continuous at the edges. A Walls system gets a frame instead.\nNo axes, no labels, no scale bar: the frame is the box, and its width is the box side. margin widens the frame beyond the box on every side, which is how a rotated view of a periodic packing is drawn without cutting its corners off; with a margin the wrapped copies are not drawn, because the frame no longer ends at the box edge.","title":"What a frame shows"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/rendering/#The-rotation-marker","page":"Images and movies","text":"render_frame! reads system.friction: when the system carries friction it draws a thin dark line through every particle's diameter, at that particle's own rotation angle, so rotation and rolling are visible in a still as well as in a movie. The line is body-fixed: in 2D its angle is the rotation angle, and in 3D it is the particle's x diameter turned by the rotation vector with Rodrigues' formula and then projected on the image plane. A sphere whose diameter points at the camera shows a foreshortened line, and below a sixth of the diameter the frame draws a small dot instead, because the line would vanish.\n\nPass markers = true or markers = false to render_frame! or to render_packing to force the lines on or off; the default nothing follows the friction setting.\n\nrender_packing(\"packing.ppm\", packing; size = 720)\nSoftPack.render_packing(\"packing.ppm\", packing, markers = false)","title":"The rotation marker"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/rendering/#Movies","page":"Images and movies","text":"pack(movie = Movie(\"packing.mp4\"; every = 500, size = 720, fps = 30))\n\nA Movie renders a frame every every steps (or every elapsed interval) into a temporary directory and encodes them into path when the run ends. The PPM frames are deleted afterwards unless encoding failed, in which case the directory is kept and its path is reported in packing.control.warnings. Encoding needs ffmpeg on the PATH; a run without it still packs, and the movie is skipped with a warning.","title":"Movies"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/rendering/#Cost","page":"Images and movies","text":"Rendering a 720 pixel frame of 256 disks takes a few milliseconds, so a movie every 500 steps over an 87,000 step run adds about a second to the run and one frame per 500 steps. The frames are 3 bytes per pixel before encoding; a full video of the default packing is about 1.5 MB.","title":"Cost"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/#DampedDynamics()","page":"Overview","text":"Up: algorithm\n\nDamped dynamics relaxes a packing with a physical trajectory [Agnolin2007], [Samiei2013]. Every contact carries a dashpot, the box may also carry a Stokes drag, and the equations of motion are integrated with one of three integrators. The packing comes to rest when the dissipation has removed the energy, so the algorithm stops on a force or energy test like FIRE does, but the path it took is a real trajectory that can be analyzed.\n\nThe motion is\n\nM ddotx = F - Gamma dotx - a_v M dotx\n\nwith F the elastic contact forces, Gamma the damping matrix assembled from the contact dashpots, and a_v the Stokes drag rate. Rotation gets the same treatment with the moment of inertia in place of the mass.\n\nDamped dynamics is the right tool when the damping model itself is the question, for example when comparing a contact-damping run with an experiment. It is not the quickest way to a jammed packing: the dashpots die out slowest on the soft modes of the packing, and after the compression band is reached the few free particles keep drifting, so the tail is long. In the benchmarks it did not reach 10^-4 (frictionless, N = 32) in 5times10^5 steps, while FIRE does in about 10^5. Use a looser tolerance or StokesDrag(during = :always) for production frictional runs.","title":"DampedDynamics()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/#Options","page":"Overview","text":"Option Default Meaning\nintegrator :centered_velocity_verlet how the equations are stepped; see integrator\ndamping ContactDamping() the contact dashpots; see damping\ndrag nothing optional Stokes drag; see drag","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/#Example","page":"Overview","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    algorithm = DampedDynamics(\n        damping = ContactDamping(\n            normal = 0.98,\n            tangential = 0.98,\n        ),\n        drag = StokesDrag(\n            velocity = 1.0,\n            during = :compression,\n        ),\n        integrator = :centered_velocity_verlet,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 64,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.converged","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/#References","page":"Overview","text":"[Agnolin2007] Ivana Agnolin, Jean-Noël Roux (2007). Internal states of model isotropic granular packings. I. Assembling process, geometry, and contact networks. Physical Review E 76, 061302. doi:10.1103/physreve.76.061302.\n[Samiei2013] K. Samiei, B. Peters, M. Bolten, A. Frommer (2013). Assessment of the potentials of implicit integration method in discrete element modelling of granular matter. Computers & Chemical Engineering 49, 183–193. doi:10.1016/j.compchemeng.2012.10.009.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/rattlers_file/#rattlers_file","page":"rattlers_file","text":"Up: write_packing()\n\nThe main file holds the particles that remain after the rattler rule. The second file is a SoftPack choice. rattlers_file = true writes a second file with every particle and a rattler flag, so the removed ones are not lost:\n\nThe second path is derived from the first: packing.h5 gives packing_rattlers.h5 in the same directory, and a path without an extension gets _rattlers at the end. With format = :csv the second file follows the same rule.\n\nDefault: false","title":"rattlers_file"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/rattlers_file/#Choices","page":"rattlers_file","text":"Choice Default\nfalse default\ntrue ","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"write_packing/rattlers_file/#Example","page":"rattlers_file","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(particles_count = 64, seed = 1)\nwrite_packing(\n    \"packing.h5\",\n    packing;\n    rattlers_file = true,\n)","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"#SoftPack","page":"Home","text":"SoftPack is a Julia package for generating jammed packings of soft particles for granular and soft-matter simulations. The goal is one well-documented, reproducible place to make packings, so that packings used across studies are built the same way and described by the same metadata.\n\nStatus: the engine implements every setting of the design; pre-release. The pages describe the interface.","title":"SoftPack"},{"category":"section","location":"#Start-here","page":"Home","text":"SoftPack has two functions you call. Each page opens with every default written out and goes deeper only as far as you follow the links.\n\npack(): create a packing. It starts from the call with every default, then lists every option, then each option's choices, then each choice's own settings.\nwrite_packing(): save a packing to disk. It starts from the call with every default, then each option.","title":"Start here"},{"category":"section","location":"#Benchmarks","page":"Home","text":"Benchmarks: how SoftPack compares with the jamming and friction literature, with figures, tables and a packing movie.","title":"Benchmarks"},{"category":"section","location":"#Reference","page":"Home","text":"Naming rules: how keywords and types are named.\nUnits and length unit: the units every page uses.\nTheory pages: models, derivations and worked numbers behind the defaults.\nNotation: every symbol, its meaning, and its units under each contact law.\nReferences: the full citation list, with the pages that use each source.","title":"Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"#Roadmap","page":"Home","text":"Roadmap: planned particle shapes (cylinders, user-defined shapes) and what the design keeps open for them.","title":"Roadmap"},{"category":"section","location":"#Conventions","page":"Home","text":"Notation follows Notation: gamma is a dashpot coefficient (force per velocity), rates are r and a, and c is never damping.\nEquations are LaTeX: inline $...$, display $$...$$.\nSources are cited by key, for example [Somfai2005], with the full citation at the bottom of the pages that carry a reference list; the complete list is on the References page.\nQuotes are copied verbatim from the sources' full texts and keep the sources' own symbols.\nSoftPack choice marks a decision with no literature source, and SoftPack note marks our own derivation, estimate or unit conversion.","title":"Conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-these-pages-live","page":"Home","text":"The pages are Markdown files in docs/src/ of the SoftPack repository, and this site is built from them with Documenter.jl. The design document states each decision with its reasons and sources.","title":"Where these pages live"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/PackParams/#PackParams()","page":"PackParams()","text":"Up: pack()\n\nA PackParams is one set of settings, built from the same keywords pack() takes. It exists so a set of settings can be built once, inspected, reused, and passed to pack:\n\npack(params) validates the settings and runs exactly like pack(; kwargs...) with the same keywords. The result's packing.settings is the PackParams of the run, and a restart compares its stored copy with the new keywords.\n\nThe constructor stores the settings; pack(params) validates them before the run, so an invalid setting raises an error at the call rather than after a minute of packing.","title":"PackParams()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/PackParams/#The-keywords","page":"PackParams()","text":"Keyword Default Sets\ndimension 2 disks or spheres\nparticles_count 256 the number of particles\nparticles_size_distribution Bidisperse() how diameters are drawn\nparticles_diameters nothing an explicit list of diameters\ncontact Hookean() the normal force law\nfriction nothing the tangential model\nmass EqualMass() the masses\ntarget Pressure(1e-4) the pressure to compress to\nboundary Periodic() periodic box or walls\ninitial_state Dilute(0.25) where the run starts\nprotocol IsotropicCompression(1e-2) how the box shrinks\nconvergence RelativeForce(1e-8) when a minimization is done\npressure_tolerance 1e-4 the width of the pressure band\nsteps_max 10^7 the step cap\nfailure_action :warn what a failed run does\nrestart_packing nothing continue from a file\nrestart_file nothing save the state during the run\nalgorithm FIRE() the minimizer or dynamics\ntimestep CollisionTime() the starting step\nrattlers ContactCount() which particles are rattlers\nseed 1 the random seed\nsnapshots nothing store positions at intervals\nmovie nothing render a movie","title":"The keywords"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/PackParams/#Example","page":"PackParams()","text":"using SoftPack\n\nparams = PackParams(\n    dimension = 3,\n    particles_count = 512,\n    seed = 7,\n)\npacking = pack(params)\npacking.packing_fraction","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/damping/ViscousRate/#ViscousRate()","page":"ViscousRate()","text":"Up: damping\n\nThe dashpot is a fixed rate per unit mass [Santos2024]:\n\ngamma_n = M_n r_n qquad gamma_t = M_t r_t\n\nwith r in inverse time. Because the coefficient does not depend on the stiffness, it does not change when the contact law or the stiffness changes, which makes it the right choice when you want a relaxation time you control rather than one the contact sets.\n\nThe default rate is 0.5 for both directions, a damping time of two in reduced units.","title":"ViscousRate()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/damping/ViscousRate/#Options","page":"ViscousRate()","text":"Option Default Meaning\nnormal 0.5 r_n, the normal rate\ntangential 0.5 r_t, the tangential rate\nattractive false whether the dashpot can pull\ncoulomb_limit :elastic which normal force caps the tangential dashpot","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/damping/ViscousRate/#Example","page":"ViscousRate()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    algorithm = DampedDynamics(\n        damping = ViscousRate(\n            normal = 0.5,      # rate r_n\n            tangential = 0.5,  # rate r_t\n        ),\n    ),\n    particles_count = 64,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.converged","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/damping/ViscousRate/#References","page":"ViscousRate()","text":"[Santos2024] A. P. Santos, Ishan Srivastava, Leonardo E. Silbert, Jeremy B. Lechman, Gary S. Grest (2024). Protocol-dependent frictional granular jamming simulations: cyclical, compression, and expansion. Frontiers in Soft Matter 3, 1326756. doi:10.3389/frsfm.2023.1326756.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/protocol/#protocol","page":"Overview","text":"Up: Available options\n\nHow the box is brought to the target [Agnolin2007]. The protocol changes the box, then lets the algorithm restore force balance, then asks whether the pressure is where it should be; the answer decides whether the next step grows, shrinks or stays.\n\nDefault: IsotropicCompression(strain_step = 1e-2)","title":"protocol"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/protocol/#Choices","page":"Overview","text":"Choice Default\nIsotropicCompression() default","title":"Choices"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/protocol/#Example","page":"Overview","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    protocol = IsotropicCompression(\n        strain_step = 5e-3,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.strain_step_final","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/protocol/#References","page":"Overview","text":"[Agnolin2007] Ivana Agnolin, Jean-Noël Roux (2007). Internal states of model isotropic granular packings. I. Assembling process, geometry, and contact networks. Physical Review E 76, 061302. doi:10.1103/physreve.76.061302.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/naming/#Naming-rules","page":"Naming rules","text":"Up: SoftPack\n\nThe interface follows one pattern, so a setting can be guessed instead of looked up [JuliaStyle].\n\nTypes are CamelCase. A setting is a type, and its name says what it is: Hookean, CundallStrack, DampedDynamics, RestartFile.\nKeywords and fields are snake_case. particles_count, pressure_tolerance, friction_coefficient.\nObject, then property. A keyword is named for the thing it sets and then the aspect: particles_count, particles_size_distribution, particles_diameters. The plural object comes first, because the keyword is a field of the run, not a verb.\nBooleans read as statements. friction = false on a wall is read as \"wall friction is off\"; the keyword is not no_friction.\nNo abbreviations and no units in names. stiffness, not k; strain_step, not deps. A name never carries a unit, because the run is in reduced units and a name with a unit would lie when the unit changes.\nFunctions are verbs. write_packing, find_rattlers, render_frame!. A function that changes its argument ends in !.\nA page is named after the thing it documents. The page for a keyword is the keyword (dimension.md); the page for a choice is the constructor (Hookean.md).\n\nThe rules are enforced by review, not by the compiler; a new setting that breaks them should be renamed before it is merged.","title":"Naming rules"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/naming/#References","page":"Naming rules","text":"[JuliaStyle] The Julia Project. Style Guide, The Julia Language manual. https://docs.julialang.org/en/v1/manual/style-guide/. Accessed 2026-11-18.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/KineticEnergy/#KineticEnergy()","page":"KineticEnergy()","text":"Up: convergence\n\nA test for dynamics rather than minimization [Agnolin2007], [Xiong2019]: the packing must be at rest,\n\nfrac1Nsum_i left(tfrac12 m_i v_i^2 + tfrac12 I_i omega_i^2right)  varepsilon  P_texttarget  d_textref^D\n\nand the ordinary RelativeForce test must hold at its default tolerance. The right-hand side is the energy scale of the target pressure, so a run at a lower pressure is asked for a correspondingly lower kinetic energy.\n\nThe rotational term was fixed for 3D. The test exists for damped dynamics, where the velocities are physical and a packing can be mechanically balanced while one particle still drifts. FIRE manages its velocities itself, so with FIRE the plain force test is the natural one.\n\nwarning: Needs drag on: a particle touching nothing is never damped\nContact dashpots enter through the stiffness matrix (boldsymbolGamma = bmathbf K_0, §8 of the design), so a particle with no contact is a zero mode of mathbf K_0 and its velocity does not decay at all. With drag = nothing a single free particle therefore keeps drifting and this test never passes, however good the packing is. Add drag = StokesDrag(...), which damps every particle, or use a force-based test, which does not see a uniform drift.","title":"KineticEnergy()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/KineticEnergy/#Options","page":"KineticEnergy()","text":"Option Default Meaning\ntolerance 1e-8 varepsilon, relative to the pressure energy scale","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/KineticEnergy/#Example","page":"KineticEnergy()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    algorithm = DampedDynamics(),  # velocities are physical\n    convergence = KineticEnergy(\n        tolerance = 1e-8,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 64,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.converged","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/convergence/KineticEnergy/#References","page":"KineticEnergy()","text":"[Agnolin2007] Ivana Agnolin, Jean-Noël Roux (2007). Internal states of model isotropic granular packings. I. Assembling process, geometry, and contact networks. Physical Review E 76, 061302. doi:10.1103/physreve.76.061302.\n[Xiong2019] Fansheng Xiong, Philip Wang, Abram H. Clark, Thibault Bertrand, Nicholas T. Ouellette, Mark D. Shattuck, Corey S. O’Hern (2019). Comparison of shear and compression jammed packings of frictional disks. Granular Matter 21, 109. doi:10.1007/s10035-019-0964-9.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/steps_max/#steps_max","page":"steps_max","text":"Up: Available options\n\nThe cap on the number of integration steps in one call is a SoftPack choice. A run that reaches it stops with stop_reason = :steps_max and converged = false, and the packing it has reached is returned; failure_action decides whether that is a warning or an error.\n\nThe cap exists so a run that cannot converge ends instead of running forever. It is not a target: a run that converges uses whatever it needs, and the default 10^7 is high enough that a normal run never reaches it. The default packing needs 87,000 steps.\n\nIf a run does reach the cap, the useful next steps are to look at the pressure and the residual in the result, loosen the tolerance, or continue from a restart file with a higher cap.\n\nDefault: 10^7","title":"steps_max"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/steps_max/#Options","page":"steps_max","text":"Value Meaning\nany integer the maximum number of steps","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/steps_max/#Example","page":"steps_max","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    steps_max = 10^7,  # cap per call\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.steps","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/damping/ContactDamping/#ContactDamping()","page":"ContactDamping()","text":"Up: damping\n\nThe dashpot coefficient follows the contact time [Agnolin2007], [Gilabert2007]:\n\ngamma_n = 2 zeta_n sqrtk_n M_n qquad gamma_t = 2 zeta_t sqrtk_t M_t\n\nwith M_n the reduced mass of the pair and M_t the tangential effective mass. The default ratio is zeta = 098 for both directions, close to critical damping: the contact is almost critically damped, so it neither rings nor crawls.\n\nThe tangential mass includes the particle's rotation, M_t = M_n(1 + 1kappa) with kappa = 12 for a disk and 25 for a sphere, because the tangential spring turns the particles as well as translating them. With equal masses the coefficient is proportional to sqrtk for both directions, so ContactDamping on a single contact law gives a stiffness-proportional damping matrix, Gamma propto K, and the dashpots scale with the stiffness when the stiffness changes.\n\nWith friction, a contact that is sliding drops its tangential dashpot: the tangential force is on the Coulomb cap, so a tangential velocity cannot do work against it.","title":"ContactDamping()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/damping/ContactDamping/#Options","page":"ContactDamping()","text":"Option Default Meaning\nnormal 0.98 zeta_n, the normal damping ratio\ntangential 0.98 zeta_t, the tangential damping ratio\ntangential_mass :with_rotation M_t includes rotation; :pair uses the pair mass\nattractive false whether the dashpot can pull\ncoulomb_limit :elastic which normal force caps the tangential dashpot","title":"Options"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/damping/ContactDamping/#Example","page":"ContactDamping()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    algorithm = DampedDynamics(\n        damping = ContactDamping(\n            normal = 0.98,      # zeta_n\n            tangential = 0.98,  # zeta_t\n        ),\n    ),\n    particles_count = 64,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.converged","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/DampedDynamics/damping/ContactDamping/#References","page":"ContactDamping()","text":"[Agnolin2007] Ivana Agnolin, Jean-Noël Roux (2007). Internal states of model isotropic granular packings. I. Assembling process, geometry, and contact networks. Physical Review E 76, 061302. doi:10.1103/physreve.76.061302.\n[Gilabert2007] F. A. Gilabert, J.-N. Roux, A. Castellanos (2007). Computer simulation of model cohesive powders: Influence of assembling procedure and contact laws on low consolidation states. Physical Review E 75, 011303. doi:10.1103/physreve.75.011303.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/FIRE/#FIRE()","page":"FIRE()","text":"Up: algorithm\n\nFIRE (fast inertial relaxation engine) is a minimization that behaves like a damped oscillator with a memory [Bitzek2006], [Guenole2020]. Each step moves the particles with a semi-implicit Euler step, then mixes the velocity toward the direction of the force:\n\nv leftarrow (1 - alpha) v + alpha  v  fracFF\n\nwith alpha starting at the mixing coefficient. When the power W = F cdot v (the FIRE papers write P, which SoftPack uses for the pressure) is positive the algorithm is going downhill, so the step is allowed to grow and alpha is shrunk; when the power is negative it is going uphill, so the step is cut and alpha is reset.","title":"FIRE()"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/FIRE/#Why-it-is-the-default","page":"FIRE()","text":"FIRE therefore needs no tuning of its time step for a new packing, which is why it is the default.\n\nThe first ten parameters are the standard FIRE 2.0 scheme, with the LAMMPS values; integrator, displacement_max, damping and drag are SoftPack additions. You rarely need to touch them.\n\nOption Default Meaning\ntimestep_max 10.0 upper bound of the adaptive step, in multiples of the starting step\ntimestep_min 0.02 lower bound; a run that reaches it is stuck\ndelay 20 steps of downhill before the step is allowed to grow\ntimestep_grow 1.1 factor applied to the step on a downhill step\ntimestep_shrink 0.5 factor applied on an uphill step\nmixing_coefficient 0.25 the starting alpha\nmixing_shrink 0.99 factor applied to alpha on each downhill step\nuphill_max 2000 uphill steps tolerated before the run stops with :uphill_max\ninertia_correction true whether an uphill move steps back half a step (LAMMPS halfstepback)\ndelay_initial true whether the first delay steps are taken before growth\nintegrator :semi_implicit_euler the only integrator FIRE implements\ndisplacement_max 0.1 the largest displacement of one step, in reference diameters\ndamping nothing optional contact dashpots, see damping\ndrag nothing optional Stokes drag, see drag","title":"Why it is the default"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/FIRE/#Checks","page":"FIRE()","text":"The mixing is mass-weighted: the speed is sqrtsum_i m_iv_i^2 + sum_i I_iomega_i^2, the force direction is F_im_i and tau_iI_i, and the power that decides downhill is taken from the elastic forces alone, so a dashpot or drag cannot steer the minimization. With equal masses and no rotation this reduces to the unweighted rule, bit for bit.\n\nFIRE validates its own parameters at the call: timestep_min must not exceed timestep_max, timestep_grow must be at least 1, timestep_shrink must lie in (0 1, and delay and uphill_max must be positive. With damping set, the step ceiling is clamped to the damped stability limit measured from the packing's stiffest contact. FIRE with friction runs but warns that the combination is non-standard; the rotational mixing is provisional.","title":"Checks"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/FIRE/#Stopping","page":"FIRE()","text":"FIRE stops when the convergence test holds, when steps_max is reached, or when it has spent uphill_max steps without finding a downhill direction. The last case is reported as stop_reason = :uphill_max; it usually means the packing is stuck on a saddle and a restart with a different seed or a looser tolerance is the way forward.","title":"Stopping"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/FIRE/#Example","page":"FIRE()","text":"using SoftPack\n\npacking = pack(\n    algorithm = FIRE(\n        delay = 20,             # downhill steps before growth\n        timestep_grow = 1.1,\n        mixing_coefficient = 0.25,\n    ),\n    particles_count = 256,\n    seed = 1,\n)\npacking.converged","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"pack/options/algorithm/FIRE/#References","page":"FIRE()","text":"[Bitzek2006] Erik Bitzek, Pekka Koskinen, Franz Gähler, Michael Moseler, Peter Gumbsch (2006). Structural Relaxation Made Simple. Physical Review Letters 97, 170201. doi:10.1103/physrevlett.97.170201.\n[Guenole2020] Julien Guénolé, Wolfram G. Nöhring, Aviral Vaid, Frédéric Houllé, Zhuocheng Xie, Aruna Prakash, Erik Bitzek (2020). Assessment and optimization of the fast inertial relaxation engine (fire) for energy minimization in atomistic simulations and its implementation in lammps. Computational Materials Science 175, 109584. doi:10.1016/j.commatsci.2020.109584.","title":"References"}]
}
